#. unit_id: 66a1ab1e87e085a88723e38e6974b4f21031fe289f4a8d29ad28558252bd18c4
#. source_hash: e3d6f3eeb4d971ee593f9352c1408c27806b887881990577d8c5e2f49713debe
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Overview of Gmsh|index|Overview"
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 934aecb49b901810f1437cfd527924577279b5892614e06d90a0cafa79cb36b5
#. source_hash: efd2a73d5d5d06b1ea297e7bc56e3d892880e03b8f63a3001dfac7553fd8d7cd
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Overview of Gmsh|paragraph|8d90d3bd844a82d009b5a9ee2ba6300104fc662ceefb640087962c4660d62c7e"
msgid "Gmsh is a three-dimensional finite element mesh generator with a\nbuild-in CAD engine and post-processor. Its design goal is to provide a\nfast, light and user-friendly meshing tool with parametric input and\nflexible visualization capabilities."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 2f7160b7d621cf34053e7803634b947f503542b1b3766424edbb756455e83923
#. source_hash: bf4680c2cdb7114f4188910e12f75afb8d064b2adabc4f257187e3688c8e1bd1
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Overview of Gmsh|paragraph|e161e2cdc2ccad6d5f34ae83b12e783c3d21303fdd6d4fcc588000ead976b24f"
msgid "Gmsh is built around four modules (geometry, mesh, solver and\npost-processing), which can be controlled with the graphical user\ninterface (GUI; see @ref{Gmsh graphical user interface}), from the\ncommand line (@pxref{Gmsh command-line interface}), using text files\nwritten in Gmsh's own scripting language (@file{.geo} files; see\n@ref{Gmsh scripting language}), or through the C++, C, Python, Julia and\nFortran application programming interface (API; see @ref{Gmsh\napplication programming interface})."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: aad666639ab4f3aa412d1e1eae256e0cac09f6132bed4f6b6577bba1578bdf35
#. source_hash: 93a8460ee1645bbadb9b07f9e103be76af2d0fb14aeca80ab67454d23d061694
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Overview of Gmsh|paragraph|2719370f925cbac4357d5dd22baa3fa734dd6d7a35051844bb95a3a632ff5671"
msgid "A brief description of the four modules is given hereafter, before an\noverview of what Gmsh does best (... and what it is not so good at), and\nsome practical information on how to install and run Gmsh on your\ncomputer."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 86278c44b103d65a15590cf113fa1908f42f50e7d1d0034ebbe8bbd9015b8b3a
#. source_hash: baadac0a8ba33208ef8ee96bcf1269ab09edb685c3155cb7c9aa89214adf315d
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module|heading|Geometry module"
msgid "Geometry module"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 4246c9834fa20c324ce937335a0c99cdff81a9f8513d0263efe18590ae356366
#. source_hash: cf0a889621dda0c043029821119f56079672f775101e67be823454f9e7902d92
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module|paragraph|9a52f69e62bff44543c624c8fbf9127701ce66aa4ae1329dbe343befeeb6e1fd"
msgid "A model in Gmsh is defined using its Boundary Representation (BRep): a\nvolume is bounded by a set of surfaces, a surface is bounded by a series\nof curves, and a curve is bounded by two end points. Model entities are\ntopological entities, i.e., they only deal with adjacencies in the\nmodel, and are implemented as a set of abstract topological\nclasses. This BRep is extended by the definition of @emph{embedded}, or\ninternal, model entities: internal points, curves and surfaces can be\nembedded in volumes; and internal points and curves can be embedded in\nsurfaces."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 69be84c3341130d49b7d3160390b6b421f8be195c7bd00c8379fb4ee47b59d78
#. source_hash: d6927f8653f7b8b6f6781a17002ab7a0a841890522f943f44ea4eadfc56f9aed
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module|paragraph|b34a20bb0085b3e93dad5d3574ceb1f70b788c9b2f45a25c6608b86e98059a88"
msgid "The geometry of model entities can be provided by different CAD\nkernels. The two default kernels interfaced by Gmsh are the\n@emph{built-in} kernel and the @emph{OpenCASCADE} kernel. Gmsh does not\ntranslate the geometrical representation from one kernel to another, or\nfrom these kernels to some neutral representation. Instead, Gmsh\ndirectly queries the native data for each CAD kernel, which avoids data\nloss and is crucial for complex models where translations invariably\nintroduce issues linked to slightly different representations. Selecting\nthe CAD kernel in @file{.geo} scripts is done with the @code{SetFactory}\ncommand (@pxref{Geometry scripting commands}), while in the Gmsh API the\nkernel appears explicitly in all the relevant functions from the\n@code{gmsh/model} namespace, with @code{geo} or @code{occ} prefixes for\nthe built-in and OpenCASCADE kernel, respectively (@pxref{Namespace\ngmsh/model})."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 1711055d2fe7a8f847074debc4ba18c296ae7671909e7596a022cde1a9eb4333
#. source_hash: b89cd5c12e651758355e27502bbc7728aadc15b3f71aadb01c01f06f68340378
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module|paragraph|6cbb62e1fc0880c9a666d2fff99814ed4e9d4800d15c048048f6faf66223a8bf"
msgid "Entities can either be built in a @emph{bottom-up} manner (first points,\nthen curves, surfaces and volumes) with the built-in and OpenCASCADE\nkernels, or in a @emph{top-down} constructive solid geometry fashion\n(solids on which boolean operations are performed) with the OpenCASCADE\nkernel. Both methodologies can also be combined. Finally, groups of\nmodel entities (called ``physical groups'') can be defined, based on the\nelementary geometric entities. (See @ref{Elementary entities vs physical\ngroups}, for more information about how physical groups affect the way\nmeshes are saved.)"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 5c781a847436b3e270c4e65c199fb41db888a6b65eace8288726e12001bb432c
#. source_hash: 2fec03dcd72c610b156b23504ae745c7fa01338f6f14dc7e87921b5c8ee44a4f
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module|paragraph|4d9d5cb128ec2bdde66cfc18becb3d64753e8b2b6f7bb345303ac742a16ceb21"
msgid "Both model entities (also referred to as ``elementary entities'') and\nphysical groups are uniquely defined by a pair of integers: their\ndimension (0 for points, 1 for curves, 2 for surfaces, 3 for volumes)\nand their @emph{tag}, a strictly positive global identification\nnumber. Entity and group tags are unique per dimension:"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 2a933c531c3ca69bb191315810af3ad87fc7617deb25dc8e67ea993bab31351c
#. source_hash: 740d7bb987b43ddb9af39884ea5a1f867ef8a3b520dac9e9eac31cd19f5e3a06
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module|item|each point must possess a unique tag;"
msgid "each point must possess a unique tag;"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: b80ee66c389fdf5edd688688fc62f32476de949d56c7b86fec72a3793dd31ff9
#. source_hash: 61271bc80e7ca0f62abc06666025d27d60e5d5702ead301a01f755a72e502eed
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module|item|each curve must possess a unique tag;"
msgid "each curve must possess a unique tag;"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 4ad4a8f57d093d7e2131a19b43b7ca83cb953a0692d6a5ee8656f0db67be0d86
#. source_hash: 7bd742f514d7f9f740e658cc202627802961c02abb8863b32274746941035e7f
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module|item|each surface must possess a unique tag;"
msgid "each surface must possess a unique tag;"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 6124423662625a1cfcea0c88950b8222d17fcf724c37517c083c516d22447ff8
#. source_hash: 7db3d43c0bef485c735e1deeee72c66b49525fa7c6e4690b5f552ec8f910039b
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module|item|each volume must possess a unique tag."
msgid "each volume must possess a unique tag."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 406913df4ae3a519b35297f3067af7d4c2a9b15ca5b4441ed0b5dc3a2ce716de
#. source_hash: 12fb7032c3b2e9d0abdb848f9a97fa1a6b1c18c1e6db4d22d5578bbd0d06a8f1
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module|paragraph|each volume must possess a unique tag."
msgid "Zero or negative tags are reserved by Gmsh for internal use."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: eb8104623b67902bf1aa12614278b171d2c9d75f6dd5d2869e50ca2a84825a69
#. source_hash: 60878ef4766f22ee109f1b2716dea4a85bac260b556078d455dd2371bb83fd47
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module|paragraph|each volume must possess a unique tag.#2"
msgid "Model entities can be manipulated and transformed in a variety of ways\nwithin the geometry module, but operations are always performed directly\nwithin their respective CAD kernels. As explained above, there is no\ncommon internal geometrical representation: rather, Gmsh directly\nperforms the operations (translation, rotation, intersection, union,\nfragments, ...) on the native geometrical representation using each CAD\nkernel's own API.  In the same philosophy, models can be imported in the\ngeometry module through each CAD kernel's own import mechanisms. For\nexample, by default Gmsh imports STEP and IGES files through\nOpenCASCADE, which will lead to the creation of model entities with an\ninternal OpenCASCADE representation. Models represented with the\nbuilt-in CAD kernel can be serialized to disk by exporting them as\n@file{.geo_unrolled} files, while models contructed with the OpenCASCADE\nkernel can be serialized as @file{.brep} or @file{.xao} files."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: cb895658eb60f327a8b6871d86943ac50d1150f21e1162700439e02583c6ce2a
#. source_hash: aaf0837a2232dd15ef598073fa624c66613ea5a35098ff2ca65c89d0f126f5bb
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module|paragraph|each volume must possess a unique tag.#3"
msgid "The @ref{Gmsh tutorial}, starting with @ref{t1}, is the best place to\nlearn how to use the geometry module: it contains examples of increasing\ncomplexity based on both the built-in and the OpenCASCADE kernel.  Note\nthat many features of the geometry module can be used interactively in\nthe GUI (@pxref{Gmsh graphical user interface}), which is also a good\nway to learn about both Gmsh's scripting language and the API, as\nactions in the geometry module automatically append the related command\nin the input script file, and can optionally also generate input for the\nlanguages supported by the API (see the\n@code{General.ScriptingLanguages} option; this is still work-in-progress\nas of Gmsh 4.14.)"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: ddc143ad100734282553ad45c06f6f314d965f7ab981539aa29dbeeaeb7eca01
#. source_hash: 612823ba2a45344891b51cfde9507eb4577915aab05f7216103da2bce64854db
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module|paragraph|each volume must possess a unique tag.#4"
msgid "In addition to CAD-type geometrical entities, whose geometry is provided\nby a CAD kernel, Gmsh also supports @emph{discrete} model entities,\nwhich are defined by a mesh (e.g. STL). Gmsh does not perform\ngeometrical operations on such discrete entities, but they can be\nequipped with a geometry through a so-called ``reparametrization''\nprocedure@footnote{P. A. Beaufort, C. Geuzaine, J.-F. Remacle\n@emph{Automatic surface mesh generation for discrete models–A complete\nand automatic pipeline based on reparametrization}. Journal of\nComputational Physics, 417, 109575, 2020.}. The parametrization is then\nused for meshing, in exactly the same way as for CAD entities. See\n@ref{t13} for an example."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 949aa3a5b19c557ec562cdaed2d64461888eae0e6f5aa15f6556e4588a326cc5
#. source_hash: 6a48c0ab27376e250fe183e947a66aaf81b6af675051e2207224ba785526d027
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module|heading|Mesh module"
msgid "Mesh module"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 0700414efe5bb97a760fc7659954da34a47992178e438817521fecad1ec1da87
#. source_hash: 0000c5d8b17c14fb7b0b7b2bbff130d110df9c9874743af3732f101abbd834c5
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module|paragraph|b0dafa2a3265fed76ccd9e01ed10d24060347657dd4bc15f9b328a92ba092835"
msgid "A finite element mesh of a model is a tessellation of its geometry by\nsimple geometrical elements of various shapes (in Gmsh: lines,\ntriangles, quadrangles, tetrahedra, prisms, hexahedra and pyramids),\narranged in such a way that if two of them intersect, they do so along a\nface, an edge or a node, and never otherwise. This defines a so-called\nconformal mesh. The mesh module implements several algorithms to\ngenerate such meshes automatically. By default, meshes produced by Gmsh\nare considered as @emph{unstructured}, even if they were generated in a\n@emph{structured} way (e.g., by extrusion). This implies that the mesh\nelements are completely defined simply by an ordered list of their\nnodes, and that no predefined ordering relation is assumed between any\ntwo elements."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 79f74909bf3a5f36da509b1c629d755735c5aa35be8946cda45d897117c6ea84
#. source_hash: 546c284b363e125e7fb9560ef4f1a610cde04efb59ca600d417813c86954c1f7
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module|paragraph|6bc0728ee789006f70ef16ff34313389513359fd49b49470d8b5c1586d7eceab"
msgid "In order to guarantee the conformity of the mesh, mesh generation is\nperformed in a bottom-up flow: curves are discretized first; the mesh of\nthe curves is then used to mesh the surfaces; then the mesh of the\nsurfaces is used to mesh the volumes. In this process, the mesh of an\nentity is only constrained by the mesh of its boundary, unless entities\nof lower dimensions are explicitly embedded in entities of higher\ndimension. For example, in three dimensions, the triangles discretizing\na surface will be forced to be faces of tetrahedra in the final 3D mesh\nonly if the surface is part of the boundary of a volume, or if that\nsurface has been explicitly embedded in the volume. This automatically\nensures the conformity of the mesh when, for example, two volumes share\na common surface. Mesh elements are oriented according to the\ngeometrical orientation of the underlying entity.  Every meshing step is\nconstrained by a @emph{mesh size field}, which prescribes the desired\nsize of the elements in the mesh. This size field can be uniform,\nspecified by values associated with points in the geometry, or defined\nby general mesh size fields (for example related to the distance to some\nboundary, to a arbitrary scalar field defined on another mesh, etc.):\nsee @ref{Gmsh mesh size fields}. For each meshing step, all structured\nmesh directives are executed first, and serve as additional constraints\nfor the unstructured parts. (The generation and handling of conformal\nmeshes has important consequences on how meshes are stored internally in\nGmsh, and how they are accessed through the API: see @ref{Gmsh\napplication programming interface}.)"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: c32e35f7ace0e37c10b615b360fc2545881fd9dfa31dbbf18f0178c8e8896b57
#. source_hash: 0102aef55529946171ea7d4261a18d0024b4af089aaae04eda000949ccad46eb
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module|paragraph|bdca9aa34009a99900de3dedcb03e78bcbfecc1603aadc52afe098bba4e091a9"
msgid "Gmsh's mesh module regroups several 1D, 2D and 3D meshing algorithms:"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: b50884ec3d7f665535a24b0ba65249cedeb3f766c17d64508ba7207543b7f3e1
#. source_hash: 60208f4ff8e81de552f62d8f1687c3fd2a0ec7060c555b205566c97dfb0f7742
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module|paragraph|05ade702e6481bad706ed60aff3bd9aed56bcde4105843381efbf47af91f4d63"
msgid "The 2D @emph{unstructured} algorithms generate triangles and/or\nquadrangles (when recombination commands or options are used). The 3D\n@emph{unstructured} algorithms generate tetrahedra, or tetrahedra and\npyramids (when the boundary mesh contains quadrangles)."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 69479a5342df8c53bdfcd488a3d118c9b78a81d9f101c141f02573602223d130
#. source_hash: 87e0a5f56b86622ea499f8bf052994f39d22ac18e4050c2396b957274f337c6f
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module|paragraph|1c67db4835355a14f4888dc89f8482b140583428466fde8b9ccb8c3a5f14d305"
msgid "The 2D @emph{structured} algorithms (transfinite and extrusion) generate\ntriangles by default, but quadrangles can be obtained by using the\nrecombination commands or options. The 3D @emph{structured} algorithms\ngenerate tetrahedra, hexahedra, prisms and pyramids, depending on the\ntype of the surface meshes they are based on."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 317d6c71a0c23630be49798fc50efc3e7d6c63e54056f02c22e21ad5b67038a1
#. source_hash: 4bf034f6348ffee0763c9425f0e855a7c70bed580a5cefaf54c43af8221ecbae
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module|paragraph|5816d98465fc25f2959fb9c864c3f0f6fa05ac2974bc227ee5d433119f32ee6a"
msgid "All meshes can be subdivided to generate fully quadrangular or fully\nhexahedral meshes with the @code{Mesh.SubdivisionAlgorithm} option\n(@pxref{Mesh options})."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 05cbeee4d5e430452591402ba7f9f633e2e83cccb6a307b3f0410a353c3ce836
#. source_hash: be69c3adc4fa988a37e97bd188bd052f076d630ca1c4d2ab826e9f6cb9263d00
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|heading|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm"
msgid "Choosing the right unstructured algorithm"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 15a7abb023c3375da546b0840c458024e28ad2e7335b146ef17321c717c4928a
#. source_hash: f48c034f79483def873c231b8b3e000efecb4b54c5118049cba15bbea41689e4
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|b3de20c0fc08a1217802c28ed4d0cc9ffff5c2ee82db3190514080b5f8f68d45"
msgid "Gmsh provides a choice between several 2D and 3D unstructured\nalgorithms. Each algorithm has its own advantages and disadvantages."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: ead74d832e0f9aa254bbcd39ba1d90dc6b6db6e43d33e02ed104d8b7c2639aed
#. source_hash: f06ed755a6073005b5b0a0a3a3b93f665cb8f0b8bd37811d896ed984b49b28a5
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|b49421f31a01af6f4eec29b7f2dcd1a1ed73d17d2de750554730911a0ce223df"
msgid "For all 2D unstructured algorithms a Delaunay mesh that contains all the\npoints of the 1D mesh is initially constructed using a\ndivide-and-conquer algorithm@footnote{R. A. Dwyer, @emph{A simple\ndivide-and-conquer algorithm for computing Delaunay triangulations in\nO(n log n) expected time}, In Proceedings of the second annual symposium\non computational geometry, Yorktown Heights, 2--4 June 1986.}. Missing\nedges are recovered using edge swaps@footnote{N. P. Weatherill,\n@emph{The integrity of geometrical boundaries in the two-dimensional\nDelaunay triangulation}, Commun. Appl. Numer. Methods 6(2),\npp. 101--109, 1990.}. After this initial step several algorithms can be\napplied to generate the final mesh:"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 8d74673d19c3b81f1a28ee1a8f16f0ebef69a37236e9f1dfaf8f5d293b55e7a7
#. source_hash: 586030b8696c30dedd5cb7ef0c8e178019e336f771d092b961a95da62d9db0a9
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|9eea19e937a9c3542582e2b030ade7408a17b81a2d6ac0d605e037f364b956dc"
msgid "The ``MeshAdapt'' algorithm@footnote{C. Geuzaine and J.-F. Remacle,\n@emph{Gmsh: a three-dimensional finite element mesh generator with\nbuilt-in pre- and post-processing facilities}, International Journal for\nNumerical Methods in Engineering 79(11), pp. 1309--1331, 2009.} is based\non local mesh modifications. This technique makes use of edge swaps,\nsplits, and collapses: long edges are split, short edges are collapsed,\nand edges are swapped if a better geometrical configuration is obtained."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 7bf8f61ada09c7778197625be808a923c39f52d31a2c8f1dbdd9d22bc164a927
#. source_hash: 0f34dbabcfb68c18d36e0d638c3b56dcd72b6a7aff7153dcc3fc58dbe39da6df
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|c4ecc603fdbf0ebfce3aa7b3e0c11cb8740a715ad4a2713628ccd087fd8b56ee"
msgid "The ``Delaunay'' algorithm is inspired by the work of the GAMMA team at\nINRIA@footnote{P.-L. George and P. Frey, @emph{Mesh generation}, Hermes,\nLyon, 2000.}. New points are inserted sequentially at the circumcenter\nof the element that has the largest adimensional circumradius. The mesh\nis then reconnected using an anisotropic Delaunay criterion."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 7a3d8fc0839671962718bc5e0c1a0531f7a280cfa76391be35db31adeeea409d
#. source_hash: 1953cfa9f8b1013db9908890f146906e1ed029165cba02bfebea49aceadfaeed
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|2fc712849fbc70e27f31bcfc640872fd724bce8fcfd9c9342a38bc27ff0e3816"
msgid "The ``Frontal-Delaunay'' algorithm is inspired by the work of\nS. Rebay@footnote{S. Rebay, @emph{Efficient unstructured mesh generation\nby means of Delaunay triangulation and Bowyer-Watson algorithm},\nJ. Comput.  Phys. 106, pp. 25--138, 1993.}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: fc79b478e08804ab97a9ec6b1dafd0d1e95280d3428a0d2f0d4d515cdc164b1e
#. source_hash: 5afea5cdaf73f270d230f3106256dc0a820a173a202271c9dda1a839f34ce5a0
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|518d636eeac279a213c96a087dfb51987e5bea713846e2e645e50b56b26de66f"
msgid "Other experimental algorithms with specific features are also\navailable. In particular, ``Frontal-Delaunay for\nQuads''@footnote{J.-F. Remacle, F. Henrotte, T. Carrier-Baudouin,\nE. Béchet, E. Marchandise, C. Geuzaine and T. Mouton, @emph{A frontal\nDelaunay quad mesh generator using the Linf norm}, International Journal\nfor Numerical Methods in Engineering, 94(5), pp. 494-512, 2013.} is a\nvariant of the ``Frontal-Delaunay'' algorithm aiming at generating\nright-angle triangles suitable for recombination; and\n``BAMG''@footnote{F. Hecht, @emph{BAMG: bidimensional anisotropic mesh\ngenerator}, User Guide, INRIA, Rocquencourt, 1998.} allows to generate\nanisotropic triangulations."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 037b9ea0ce9b95ac00e46eec5d0e9973b2fab62a595ab31bf09917b89b68e81f
#. source_hash: c5071ae595bbe467d29ab2b44875d1f8eea879166b73c396075e2c9aca40b638
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|3b51ef187359c3a89a5fa1a87120c8310395bf055e57d54741ae83be7b8e2c69"
msgid "For very complex curved surfaces the ``MeshAdapt'' algorithm is the most\nrobust. When high element quality is important, the ``Frontal-Delaunay''\nalgorithm should be tried. For very large meshes of plane surfaces the\n``Delaunay'' algorithm is the fastest; it usually also handles complex\nmesh size fields better than the ``Frontal-Delaunay''.  When the\n``Delaunay'' or ``Frontal-Delaunay'' algorithms fail, ``MeshAdapt'' is\nautomatically triggered. The ``Automatic'' algorithm uses ``Delaunay''\nfor plane surfaces and ``MeshAdapt'' for all other surfaces."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: e658673551c7723f04078ad7ffcb63befac7afc9ce9681dae75916284e8aa5d4
#. source_hash: ba77aa6541b95413f01aa1be55116c0d5ba763f9514cc253003a1649ec40d994
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|c521ae2c953a8f4e738d6fce04987315c65c477681d1e2e26a957e5760a6c80a"
msgid "Several 3D unstructured algorithms are also available:"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: d7f5528f59b3b13c272fbaf84f651d92ab85969bd02bfd2a22254ed85c402f78
#. source_hash: d6e12ffbfb026deeb2b797d86a9fdaf00709580718e0811af0385291483fd82e
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|d39c25db28d5040a53a08db14917c5b47a9236c164ae442d7174f006cb295124"
msgid "The ``Delaunay'' algorithm is split into three separate steps. First, an\ninitial mesh of the union of all the volumes in the model is performed,\nwithout inserting points in the volume. The surface mesh is then\nrecovered using H. Si's boundary recovery algorithm Tetgen/BR. Then a\nthree-dimensional version of the 2D Delaunay algorithm described above\nis applied to insert points in the volume to respect the mesh size\nconstraints."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 9469d87772dc2a0c4d9539c8b8cc44d7aa10ff7b0476951d4eaa4f58eb607d23
#. source_hash: 4529ed1817626ad3da592633fab0be08f3b83799a84af4fcf05f3c89180c5ba8
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|3ecfde6159afa970c15508cf31971fa6bf75541b927a8d850d9859f1981d3232"
msgid "The ``Frontal'' algorithm uses J. Schoeberl's Netgen algorithm\n@footnote{J. Schoeberl, @emph{Netgen, an advancing front 2d/3d-mesh\ngenerator based on abstract rules}, Comput. Visual. Sci., 1, pp. 41--52,\n1997.}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 440013654ef712b354f76cf5cee6ff9c8aa98c235899fd288d420e2486055fd4
#. source_hash: 802f36e00f8884de3704378970aea1b308304119bc157d3ee9808d2ee22fd289
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|1db5aa6d76ece5558ac912262e164116d1ed63364ea557c2767397225253429a"
msgid "The ``HXT'' algorithm@footnote{C. Marot, J. Pellerin and J.‐F. Remacle,\n@emph{One machine, one minute, three billion tetrahedra}, International\nJournal for Numerical Methods in Engineering 117.9, pp 967-990, 2019.}\nis a new efficient and parallel reimplementaton of the Delaunay\nalgorithm."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 4730390c80e4f2121dc729d4fd6d145ea04b2384c51dec8d3a1f344a53c73c79
#. source_hash: faa784ee1537d7ae86de9e80c1a93ed81539a5531e9c234658c58d56a0c890ae
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|dde4868493079ac7909967398e3bc88de374d2dd59ba555c89cbb24bf9e6d4b4"
msgid "Other experimental algorithms with specific features are also available.\nIn particular, ``MMG3D''@footnote{C. Dobrzynski, @emph{MMG3D: user\nguide}, INRIA, 2012.}  allows to generate anisotropic\ntetrahedralizations."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: cdfcdfe541afff575a7e0e4f04c7bf26f48f846373301f50e0cda12560f457a3
#. source_hash: 69b106f27b008cb806fc90c291c0e3a9f93ffe0086e757bd025eda0f1d7d271a
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|ef7579bd426342f65c95b952448b03278802f868f685eb07bfe286b786391430"
msgid "The ``Delaunay'' algorithm is currently the most robust and is the only\none that supports the automatic generation of hybrid meshes with\npyramids. Embedded model entities and general mesh size fields\n(@pxref{Specifying mesh element sizes}) are currently only supported by\nthe ``Delaunay'' and ``HXT'' algorithms."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 4dc3758ef4d1445d8f83f07604c98cc6f7632d30c68ec7b196a174410aa69061
#. source_hash: f5ffa97fb2886be9bc5dd063fa82b36d8747ea5cc19a7f8897649ade3c436af9
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|a9a9b5f59d8bb579175546856efc7c8b1464351c3a43b5ff063a4af1593381d4"
msgid "When Gmsh is configured with OpenMP support (@pxref{Compiling the source\ncode}), most of the meshing steps can be performed in parallel:"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: f08e07a02573d492a726c317480972e40f494c53e1251b852278b3ebba62cda9
#. source_hash: fcdd1c3a2efc283b10631fd44278caf36310d6a15e14839575939ced0d5d0616
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|77dc244229c6bc2daa11e5f26f78c41c4725ae35c2c22212eafa33ccccfc993f"
msgid "1D and 2D meshing is parallelized using a coarse-grained approach,\ni.e. curves (resp. surfaces) are each meshed sequentially, but several\ncurves (resp. surfaces) can be meshed at the same time."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 3f8ba93407afbeb24bf18e529ee6377f98e930c30064a38ece4f1f917fb18558
#. source_hash: fd890da4143aaf5622b45198f7047c34e400a52fa26c08531ff32bddacfaa2c7
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|09f9748bd77de46caa937721c4cc4170b1e87d4441f8d454584447c1d375227b"
msgid "3D meshing using HXT is parallelized using a fine-grained approach,\ni.e. the actual meshing procedure for a single volume is done is\nparallel."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: f3c2786bf3ca4b4e6234c9f556f427a90532248fa5a0e476b69ba31529a20a26
#. source_hash: 9da38a55d3ecb03f10c72e310fb8d018346b33ad5bfd6221d497cac6d38def0b
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Choosing the right unstructured algorithm|paragraph|87ffd0e7e3ffb20675b5522db2971f8200f8b7167250cc694abe235bc0a8e093"
msgid "The number of threads can be controlled with the @code{-nt} flag on the\ncommand line (@pxref{Gmsh command-line interface}), or with the\n@code{General.NumThreads}, @code{Mesh.MaxNumThreads1D},\n@code{Mesh.MaxNumThreads2D} and @code{Mesh.MaxNumThreads3D} options (see\n@ref{General options} and @ref{Mesh options})."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 27001a9897b410a9e51fc12706ae3e16a8efd7201b2dc78854a007a9e3916950
#. source_hash: d20eed660ff627dbec5c7eaea42690b98ab429b41727a8570975335600e591b8
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Specifying mesh element sizes|heading|Specifying mesh element sizes"
