msgid "with"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: d7fcbd14d0447c99651a60d4522e9dc84406438ef743823b17dd283cde5ef25a
#. source_hash: 8b8fb52abfe310ca29021112a11e663e8ed69236b4fc64b535cce4652903b35d
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Floating point expressions|paragraph|0bc323e3a4456917c487175107c258b2b91765260ee2a159ad85a10e8f5742ff"
msgid "The second case in this last definition permits to create a list\ncontaining the range of numbers comprised between two\n@w{@var{expression}s}, with a unit incrementation step. The third case\nalso permits to create a list containing the range of numbers comprised\nbetween two @w{@var{expression}s}, but with a positive or negative\nincrementation step equal to the third @var{expression}. The fourth,\nfifth and sixth cases permit to reference an expression list\n(parentheses can also be used instead of brackets).  @code{Unique} sorts\nthe entries in the list and removes all duplicates.  @code{Abs} takes\nthe absolute value of all entries in the list. @code{ListFromFile} reads\na list of numbers from a file. @code{LinSpace} and @code{LogSpace}\nconstruct lists using linear or logarithmic spacing. The next two cases\npermit to reference an expression sublist (whose elements are those\ncorresponding to the indices provided by the @var{expression-list}).\nThe next cases permit to retrieve the indices of entities created\nthrough geometrical transformations, extrusions and boolean operations\n(see @ref{Transformations}, @ref{Extrusions} and @ref{Boolean\noperations})."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 4b19cd7d3dc97e06db9ca9690a368b6a70e1a82388becbaa46c28b14a18c7c81
#. source_hash: 6edf865d2cab90113bbe61959b3494daff63a6016dd210385cab10e1047ae3b3
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Floating point expressions|paragraph|ab42f1c5304106538706db5739e90a8aeb41b9812bc158ccd3757094615cdbcc"
msgid "The next two cases allow to retrieve entities in a given bounding box,\nor get the bounding box of a given entity, with the bounding box\nspecified as (X min, Y min, Z min, X max, Y max, Z max). Beware that the\norder of coordinates is different than in the @code{BoundingBox} command\nfor the scene: see @ref{Other general commands}. @code{Parametric\nBoundingBox} retrieves the parametric bounds (U min, U max) and (U min,\nV min, U max, V max) or a curve or a surface,\nrespectively. @code{Parametric Point} retrieves the parametric\ncoordinates U, V of a point on a surface. @code{Normal Surface} returns\nthe normal to a surface evaluated at the specified parametric\ncoordinates."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 19d2fcaad14a8fe357a726eb134c8c0c283ed02048123abc37718814ba809b7c
#. source_hash: 31f7a59104b345585deb31a23200c4a57130176e682eac775fc3e1ec25514cbb
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Floating point expressions|paragraph|ea0bf479cb7e6d640d6a950e6c345503279318835afcdb9b7f4365f921e0f734"
msgid "The last cases permit to retrieve the mass, the center of mass or the\nmatrix of inertia of an entity, the coordinates of a given geometry\npoint (@pxref{Points}), the elementary entities making up physical\ngroups, and the tags of all (physical or elementary) points, curves,\nsurfaces or volumes in the model. @value{SYNCS}"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: b039888d4d5b3134d1a2a02b2a6d2c40b1bcbc089efafe820b1523e780467d7b
#. source_hash: adc34348b6e7d93caf756ce235f4b3b934a87ca77468bfe0484adaee61e498f1
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Floating point expressions|paragraph|349306e4cb7e190963552cd3b96d7a88a8851e0f04e3fb3102257bd67bb62534"
msgid "To see the practical use of such expressions, have a look at the first\ncouple of examples in @ref{Gmsh tutorial}. Note that, in order to\nlighten the syntax, you can omit the braces @code{@{@}} enclosing an\n@var{expression-list} if this @var{expression-list} only contains a\nsingle item. Also note that a braced @var{expression-list} can be\npreceded by a minus sign in order to change the sign of all the\n@w{@var{expression-list-item}s}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 3737bd9af55b7b658e917b18fe98f9186bf8274d36a7b979da21c80ee3693bd4
#. source_hash: 3d8eab8db080639897ea8f4f79232bf891c350fc1b3c0b7b1ca504d2362a4ef9
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Floating point expressions|paragraph|6afc1f9c5f46dfa13b7af543b01274d814d9fa916af686ed7177df63be38753a"
msgid "For some commands it makes sense to specify all the possible expressions\nin a list. This is achieved with @var{expression-list-or-all}, defined\nas:"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: a87bc77b6d6d4e570bf3090d4d4743c839dd6c3915df6ad2386f827e23aa25c4
#. source_hash: b9174266a71d603e6357c41da07619d382c51799720aba1487a90b82b3567e55
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Floating point expressions|paragraph|8136b6d16c7e13682afc07d4bb4a566faff4c9425cb8898f832ef7549118f9e4"
msgid "The meaning of ``all'' (@code{:}) depends on context. For example,\n@code{Curve @{ : @}} will get the ids of all the existing curves in the\nmodel, while @code{Surface @{ : @}} will get the ids of all existing\nsurfaces."
msgstr ""

