#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing scripting commands|paragraph|View \"@var{string}\" @{ @var{string} < ( @var{expression-list} ) > @{ @var{expression-list} @}; @dots{} @};"
msgid "Create a new post-processing view, named @code{\"@var{string}\"}.  This is\nan easy and quite powerful way to import post-processing data: all the\nvalues are @var{expressions}, you can embed datasets directly into your\ngeometrical descriptions (see, e.g., @ref{t4}), the data can be easily\ngenerated ``on-the-fly'' (there is no header containing @emph{a priori}\ninformation on the size of the dataset). The syntax is also very\npermissive, which makes it ideal for testing purposes."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 1f943d0dbe1341b1cd36eafd00524d72e4486b4fcc4bc12edbe0e68d0d124662
#. source_hash: efe80ca9b5412434f879223fe9a4a9e88dc3e008a1e62cc042437242f84d1d03
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing scripting commands|paragraph|View \"@var{string}\" @{ @var{string} < ( @var{expression-list} ) > @{ @var{expression-list} @}; @dots{} @};#2"
msgid "However this ``parsed format'' is read by Gmsh's script parser, which\nmakes it inefficient if there are many elements in the dataset. Also,\nthere is no connectivity information in parsed views and all the\nelements are independent (all fields can be discontinuous), so a lot of\ninformation can be duplicated. For large datasets, you should thus use\nthe mesh-based post-processing file format described in @ref{Gmsh file\nformats}, or use one of the standard formats like MED."
msgstr ""

