msgid "What is Gmsh?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: e7238de572fe082f5de60c6968b596820bdd518394e9645e52e77b9ce9a30938
#. source_hash: 9999b802ae76435d6a2f70c535863ee0ee89c8508d69b8434776bdafe14fd91e
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|The basics|paragraph|What is Gmsh?"
msgid "Gmsh is an automatic three-dimensional finite element mesh generator\nwith built-in pre- and post-processing facilities. With Gmsh you can\ncreate or import 1D, 2D and 3D geometrical models, mesh them, launch\nexternal finite element solvers and visualize solutions. Gmsh can be\nused either as a stand-alone program (graphical or not) or as a library\nto integrate in C++, C, Python, Julia or Fortran codes."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 09ccdf5c1f0dd6daf1e4b743380c6d0f58423bea870a3ca5992108fff8ca1a58
#. source_hash: c5d11f3d23a559972b17d3cad0ca894732c48e20fdd164d964af980d3297ec66
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|The basics|item|What are the terms and conditions of use?"
msgid "What are the terms and conditions of use?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 15914d073167c6f31ccbf40f6942937fa74cb19f9aec4848d6ff0ed02678e241
#. source_hash: 882c6307c7dc4984a57837cb5d5cc80db87087a440fbad8773d30d5d01db03ce
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|The basics|paragraph|What are the terms and conditions of use?"
msgid "Gmsh is distributed under the terms of the GNU General Public License,\nwith an exception to allow for easier linking with external\nlibraries. See @ref{License} for more information."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: bf2d290ec2a8807cbdb62ab022502fdf6591f9c076a9c98d298bcf2624e7ef7e
#. source_hash: dc4d6819b8250ccd72ca9b6fd164d1a55cbd6fb78c11c9e4588441eba06c5239
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|The basics|item|What does 'Gmsh' mean?"
msgid "What does 'Gmsh' mean?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 915b2ff3ee2fa4948e060216ad3bbed91b0c48c9f536f50ef3132ea9c172e9fc
#. source_hash: 7673d414748a2750a6e16f92a7c7d02c5a9d8bd6878dba08f33417204dae94e9
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|The basics|paragraph|What does 'Gmsh' mean?"
msgid "Nothing... The name was derived from a previous version called ``msh''\n(a shortcut for ``mesh''), with the ``g'' prefix added to differentiate\nit. The default mesh file format used by Gmsh still uses the @file{.msh}\nextension."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 95585e30828022016acb95d342680b99b58f3273e85ebf58a361f3414ccab583
#. source_hash: cdf2845195e4eab6dd0a2ff8f2d1a9c0004e88054cb98a447a17b7d75e500cfb
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|The basics|paragraph|What does 'Gmsh' mean?#2"
msgid "In English people tend to pronounce `Gmsh' as ``gee-mesh''."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 982851bfecfe2a91aa9b6cc7d9ccbfceb9c273ad050573c96a23b5d0961dae4a
#. source_hash: 3cd7e9724a7f55e0631a7189bc6d18e4691985bd7b82d11d66e13a48207505b7
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|The basics|item|Can I embed 'Gmsh' in my own software?"
msgid "Can I embed 'Gmsh' in my own software?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: ec0eb7d9173fefb6905633d6514a3d6b81c5e764191b565c8d19cd6c9df577e2
#. source_hash: 278bad7e0f700e91362987b5c22ed2bbd197ae372b888fe057658e99645190b5
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|The basics|paragraph|Can I embed 'Gmsh' in my own software?"
msgid "Yes, using the Gmsh API (@pxref{Gmsh application programming\ninterface}). See @ref{Copying conditions} for the licensing constraints."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: ed9b81c9cf031d319a81891506801908d50dbc32c3fb84b567a85c3136b545e0
#. source_hash: 12430a9d87701df11048a1ddf8f0742724856f11c28d7cac69ef53987b400a64
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|The basics|item|Where can I find more information?"
msgid "Where can I find more information?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: f147338acb19d09bf570dcef00f27bd6807c8c8a2930da3a95352ea89bcb6573
#. source_hash: a39bfa9344acca4d75f37d732306c0dd4feadf94ef00b0855ed0750b9526e28c
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|The basics|paragraph|Where can I find more information?"
msgid "@url{https://gmsh.info} is the primary location to obtain information\nabout Gmsh. There you will for example find the complete reference\nmanual and the @url{https://gitlab.onelab.info/gmsh/gmsh/issues,bug\ntracking database}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: c347758b638bdd03ca70cfa9fd465017913c2e3c4c47c66ce5f39e1d3aabfd0f
#. source_hash: 25bdad846beefba7bb26e35d39e6569aa62ee467ea147e64506dac7f5e9f0e79
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Installation problems|heading|Installation problems"
msgid "Installation problems"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 8da638470bbf6a570b3708d5fd75c5f8e4f5eeeb56ee343a01bee8c63b55cfe7
#. source_hash: 94b25098c0e855d00cf424995e3f67eaaac3878fb4517a56a1dbfce0127b3316
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Installation problems|item|Which OSes does Gmsh run on?"
msgid "Which OSes does Gmsh run on?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: f845cee7a1fe090d117c937834d4c886f210c6d8c56cbcde08c054491da90605
#. source_hash: c5fb772c9b9da4a76144003be7dbb6a284eff1962b6ddf50478bcf9d0e33855c
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Installation problems|paragraph|Which OSes does Gmsh run on?"
msgid "Gmsh runs on Windows, macOS, Linux and most Unix variants. Gmsh is also\navailable as part of the ONELAB package on Android and iOS tablets and\nphones."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: f4ce01f16ac2d4e1c7e918cbe11ec8760888a4e1b64fa1430b1c74ddfb328740
#. source_hash: f6b1caeef7b14753ed76f761cf2615008f43d1e69af768bbac17e2de5536d482
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Installation problems|item|Are there additional requirements to run Gmsh?"
msgid "Are there additional requirements to run Gmsh?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: c927f272847c339dd28cae5726c44b321bb85e3771012cf1e69707026f93f19d
#. source_hash: ee6df08d0bcc7fa5721ef96b832257bba4bf7a64ba9596b845bbf74e894cb038
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Installation problems|paragraph|Are there additional requirements to run Gmsh?"
msgid "You should have the OpenGL libraries installed on your system, and in\nthe path of the library loader. A free replacement for OpenGL can be\nfound at @url{http://www.mesa3d.org}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: bde80a020f97a9e99b7529f857c4c3278fa620e717cc47dfa6b1960564821622
#. source_hash: debc6bf41106284d6b8d0c2c0a4de96b794d1a45ddf5813399721ba6e4e2b031
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Installation problems|item|How do I compile Gmsh from the source code?"
msgid "How do I compile Gmsh from the source code?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 9b6c47615434fd12584685e1c4c137c31c49590ba59563d3ff39a36cfff9f6d1
#. source_hash: b869e5fb4cde74bb7e79022da0f183dc16cf8d76e830102abbd088fe29b8720b
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Installation problems|paragraph|How do I compile Gmsh from the source code?"
msgid "You need cmake (@url{http://www.cmake.org}) and a C++ compiler. See\n@ref{Compiling the source code} for more information."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: c64343af7f5434a4c37b3ba48f86da8a676842babc54006294936bc68ab4ef13
#. source_hash: a5551a494002b8a49641c071515a3a71c1da14a84e735aa69b0c30cabdadd8a8
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Installation problems|item|Where does Gmsh save its configuration files?"
msgid "Where does Gmsh save its configuration files?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 2b459c04b258890c9efbf785065efe84fa35846fa627fa22e791796f0674744e
#. source_hash: c3b4c32a9dc551840ba41c307a0233258074e4bb4b24c63050b7fc6cf10c91ad
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Installation problems|paragraph|Where does Gmsh save its configuration files?"
msgid "Gmsh will attempt to save temporary files and persistent configuration\noptions first in the @code{$GMSH_HOME} directory, then in\n@code{$APPDATA} (on Windows) or @code{$HOME} (on other OSes), then in\n@code{$TMP}, and finally in @code{$TEMP}, in that order. If none of\nthese variables are defined, Gmsh will try to save/load its\nconfiguration files from the current working directory."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: de1d41e3f30f7bb35690e28625203e0145d7d5e04e8fbc1aa1454997ec8b1e14
#. source_hash: cb3533f90f093742a4d98f4810cc9c193b0c6c9dae41814c94fbfe8cd6654754
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|heading|General questions"
msgid "General questions"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 824ae2a4cefd36a326f0268e5c3a7c24bc5d28ac1ba844d2b318c2c89266a7d2
#. source_hash: f7c14faa186fc561c4055408492b1de70be4f83a4292b250e2d63dd2db1ba3ad
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|item|Gmsh (from a binary distribution) complains about missing libraries."
msgid "Gmsh (from a binary distribution) complains about missing libraries."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 5744c3578ab3c0e0f7a7c671068117c35702f78eeaf5625fcb3146435bd690d0
#. source_hash: bfe7d21fb1436240fe5f29398e8d5ac9e543d56ae1eb6fa54fa2b2d1dc531bae
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|paragraph|Gmsh (from a binary distribution) complains about missing libraries."
msgid "On Windows, if your system complains about missing @file{OPENGL32.DLL}\nor @file{GLU32.DLL} libraries, then OpenGL is not properly installed on\nyour machine. You can download OpenGL from Microsoft's web site, or\ndirectly from @url{http://www.opengl.org}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 341b3de4ffb5bb884c24ab2ff6fb8a9aadd2049925e39b03de366ffbec392096
#. source_hash: 33a313f3a0d99824a88dec2feb17aa4b133635b44e5a29919c0257d26b163a12
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|paragraph|Gmsh (from a binary distribution) complains about missing libraries.#2"
msgid "On Unix try `ldd gmsh' (or `otool -L gmsh' on macOS) to check if all the\nrequired shared libraries are installed on your system. If not, install\nthem. If it still doesn't work, recompile Gmsh from the source code."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 607954aca1de73b235a3e11ae800ac0e32cfaca9b41fddcda9bb86245bd47990
#. source_hash: 68f69472449094cf9abc73336dc86f29173d3affded4364dee18f7334315bc3b
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|item|Gmsh keeps re-displaying its graphics when other windows partially hide the graphical window."
msgid "Gmsh keeps re-displaying its graphics when other windows partially hide the graphical window."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 0869ad4d4ef5a60cbb266c16c1f8a373c0e7b152a42a969bd724e86e1199aa25
#. source_hash: ef0fe18f7c841642e975a7b38f7f4ca72c8f682e7141623eea9cad04ddc93baa
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|paragraph|Gmsh keeps re-displaying its graphics when other windows partially hide the graphical window."
msgid "Disable opaque move in your window manager."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: a20ace23fc627a19a225b87cbc7f54c90a42d8d8a3fe46de210212f91a68825d
#. source_hash: ec70c54f97a7d8e22c9438f0eb7f30f7e8e71042d732612fa3533e59b1ca9ca1
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|item|The graphics display very slowly."
msgid "The graphics display very slowly."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 031dde284ca488439ad8220b230605fb34d7cdc3a0a0ebc88cba7581c808acb6
#. source_hash: f64c99be6b00cf7d88a818cbfa88055fc89f5df25efa9cea1a4b550437bebad0
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|paragraph|The graphics display very slowly."
msgid "Are you are executing Gmsh from a remote host (via the network)\nwithout GLX?  You should turn double buffering off (with the `-nodb'\ncommand-line switch)."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: c9a07c00fd4dc3653a29035e3e18bae2263dce7701b4412fdfb67c96544deb7f
#. source_hash: 79b86fa218e5e4f29bb2a4bb61548d5223dc724f8fdee1a2e0896e41cefdf1a7
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|item|There is an ugly ``ghost triangulation'' in the vector PostScript/PDF files generated by Gmsh!"
msgid "There is an ugly ``ghost triangulation'' in the vector PostScript/PDF files generated by Gmsh!"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: b395853c8cd49d735c3f35f9377813d8c7481e3a89501f4d6b0fc7990c144fa9
#. source_hash: cadd06c374cd954873c536f6f9d23373e808e3dd8b289d5cd5b4d6bdc7719ddb
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|paragraph|There is an ugly ``ghost triangulation'' in the vector PostScript/PDF files generated by Gmsh!"
msgid "No, there isn't. This ``ghost triangulation'' is due to the fact that most\nPostScript previewers nowadays antialias the graphic primitives when\nthey display the page on screen. (For example, in gv, you can disable\nantialising with the `State->Antialias' menu.) You should not see this\nghost triangulation in the printed output (on paper)."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 00e0620257cd33553a76ed0422ef99c828e3c373f61f5f192339685f262fffa5
#. source_hash: d69739e3b4aa663fdfd2398131f65d0c4fbe65b6687bfee52feadb9a2244fe0a
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|item|How can I save GIF, JPEG, ..., images?"
msgid "How can I save GIF, JPEG, ..., images?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: db1b2c66c9ff62416ea5759059a85eb5fd2e0bcac64a3387b4c2272e283aef9f
#. source_hash: dd4ede0c4612bc6e94b818b1ace23ce6245c0cd4430be29480c237bcd6d2a084
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|paragraph|How can I save GIF, JPEG, ..., images?"
msgid "Just choose the appropriate format in `File->Export'. By default Gmsh\nguesses the format from the file extension, so you can just type\n@file{myfile.jpg} in the dialog and Gmsh will automatically create a JPEG\nimage file."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: ba632db387a9383e78d90325a6ae4f705034e4f2689b3c8dd0b610309220e797
#. source_hash: 8db8a4555c1873acf709a139629b22bcf3cd099126dd8adb1bae6ca348fdc07f
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|item|How save high-resolution images?"
msgid "How save high-resolution images?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: bf570b6785ec740e9a54cf46a8f670a8a27dd9dac366efd4cd8f548d925e024b
#. source_hash: d1f194a12c521934dd135089d9ac764bdb1c4f96c9e3ebb9648faefbd596090e
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|paragraph|How save high-resolution images?"
msgid "You can specify the dimension in the dialog (e.g. set the width of the\nimage to 5000 pixels; leaving one dimension negative will rescale using\nthe natural aspect ratio), or through the @code{Print.Width} and\n@code{Print.Height} options. The maximum image size is graphics hardware\ndependent."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 14ce97ee5aaacd4a6e2d39607e8198ce791407672f4a0800480b7e0b777bcadb
#. source_hash: fa25399f4ee66eec12e56ddcf4b6729695485c742c0fbb92ec5a3f857e1457ef
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|item|How can I save MPEG, AVI, ..., animations?"
msgid "How can I save MPEG, AVI, ..., animations?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 750760828b7530ccf2ccad0300f99c18dafae7d6a9a97190c1c2ab9dbdb82dac
#. source_hash: d3b61b2e613262de9c98e5a033efa4328e1bc1a298a4c4afb01be2654f93418c
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|paragraph|How can I save MPEG, AVI, ..., animations?"
msgid "You can create simple MPEG animations by choosing MPEG as the format in\n`File->Export': this allows you to loop over time steps or\npost-processing data sets, or to change parameters according to\n@code{Print.Parameter}. To create fully customized animations or to use\ndifferent output formats (AVI, MP4, etc.) you should write a\nscript. Have a look at @ref{t8} or\n@url{@value{GITLAB-PREFIX}/examples/post_processing/anim.script,examples/post_processing/anim.script}\nfor some examples."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: e1a19001cc4028c9bfc38b4dbb1616fa17d1d9dac0d9a775a1dd4c2a56e6341e
#. source_hash: 9e25be8bbfa414eacbf45ccf9015e2ba3421fd14bf4bf7ba6e1689a17acf0059
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|item|Can I change values in input fields with the mouse in the GUI?"
msgid "Can I change values in input fields with the mouse in the GUI?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 62f9916d8e49bbf11b92ceb1c7f4c9a28c7adfc1883af25b56ef7bead016678d
#. source_hash: c885405bd4bef83ca932ef55c8db454f5c7a9cfb2bf0ca51593b1ddc4d5e7002
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|paragraph|Can I change values in input fields with the mouse in the GUI?"
msgid "Yes: dragging the mouse in a numeric input field slides the value! The\nleft button moves one step per pixel, the middle by `10*step', and the\nright button by `100*step'."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: c31066b60108c93a7a016bf9e09921e7c63c067d0323fb98052b8d81315b0e5b
#. source_hash: a7ce846f5949fbf41801f46e9d99eb56579e1293595049af3807c1b7838d280a
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|item|Can I copy messages to the clipboard?"
msgid "Can I copy messages to the clipboard?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 36f7449bd3fce83180a94b74b534728ec3870c5e9c79a48e591c496856005910
#. source_hash: 4ec215f498756fb46ef100e3b9350dd8fc989ba82a771f77826c4a6bc6463ce5
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|paragraph|Can I copy messages to the clipboard?"
msgid "Yes: selecting the content of an input field, or lines in the message\nconsole (`Tools->Message Console'), copies the selected text to the\nclipboard."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: c274bb21591bf49ba8aedaacf4543dff513451cb4ca84aca743c3f6f3d225057
#. source_hash: 1b11ef41c4d3d7193a316abb7f7440dd4b129b8682a8362abcd6e3023b0bf239
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|item|Graphical rendering or entity selection is very slow on Linux."
msgid "Graphical rendering or entity selection is very slow on Linux."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 47f60b2d78d3804c17cae4710018a3894e37c5630c6f454801c20b5c46a782e4
#. source_hash: bb19c8209f1bdc4f91c9dec20c5e5221ca6a1813c3683b866f845cd95faf4693
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|General questions|paragraph|Graphical rendering or entity selection is very slow on Linux."
msgid "The issue is most probably linked with your graphics card driver. Try\nsetting the environment variable @code{LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=true} to\nforce software rendering."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 63e5175e13e5dec907831a12bfb995ec11c95f34f4aa126d1fa548a859c9834d
#. source_hash: baadac0a8ba33208ef8ee96bcf1269ab09edb685c3155cb7c9aa89214adf315d
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|heading|Geometry module questions"
msgid "Geometry module"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: c0878fd786bdcae5fcdfa9cde1f6fc0a411c6c2d7ccc344caca68cb357273233
#. source_hash: 18e1608cb087628242ec220de10687976607ab2ec245553c622b49baad3a32db
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|item|Does Gmsh support trimmed NURBS surfaces?"
msgid "Does Gmsh support trimmed NURBS surfaces?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 8ab3d052b97cc6284ede15d3ebe2f114e53db7d7e743558d23e861232c94a7e7
#. source_hash: 7f6942935970627c6edbdb3474b61d5d89a5df36817b492b11f4062a86eca7b2
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|paragraph|Does Gmsh support trimmed NURBS surfaces?"
msgid "Yes, but only with the OpenCASCADE kernel."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 62d0c1186b60980fc65e1ea30781d2a0d2f36d3bc81f9021b9046c79fbca08d2
#. source_hash: 775fa74012c6c15f1c892c7ea60ac66b9b6ef3e5be6cddf0042e9f389240ac06
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|item|Gmsh is very slow when I use many transformations (Translate, Rotate, Symmetry, Extrude, etc.) with the built-in CAD kernel. What's wrong?"
msgid "Gmsh is very slow when I use many transformations (Translate, Rotate, Symmetry, Extrude, etc.) with the built-in CAD kernel. What's wrong?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: a145c0f18a2b690c9788424fe776f5070b7afd6a9b77ab1ef8dd359d90d50dc9
#. source_hash: 33687159b39347fd8b52242c8378ef61b0424e3a2b6cc863b6a9b3950461ab4a
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|paragraph|Gmsh is very slow when I use many transformations (Translate, Rotate, Symmetry, Extrude, etc.) with the built-in CAD kernel. What's wrong?"
msgid "The default behavior of Gmsh is to check and suppress all duplicate\nentities (points, curves and surfaces) each time a transformation\ncommand is issued with the built-in CAD kernel. This can slow down\nthings a lot if many transformations are performed. There are two\nsolutions to this problem:"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 6f5b91c26eefd57eb7297aba93bcb8730cb264348fbaee8a0f74f020ec24f21b
#. source_hash: 89746a807aaf2ad6f13ff2c53adb5a7dfb7655b61dad5865165db5142941b123
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|paragraph|52a0cb0bc0f9aba918a4eff337a230c55c1db9c19317cb8fd98b73e610020e3c"
msgid "you may save the serialized (``unrolled'') geometry in another file\n(using @code{gmsh file.geo -0} or exporting to ``.geo_unrolled''), and\nuse this new file for subsequent computations;"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 843cdd0e79916e1e1f5837eae99eb5b5b527067ec6d49ab6931449fb5cd5cb00
#. source_hash: dde2bf90d50f3256ef12abf11862b294cade318100039f08640a14cb51a852ed
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|paragraph|9a9b8a212abb5620b7a521c716e75ce01ac71c4ff2b0344e02189e1063f64cf8"
msgid "or you may set the @code{Geometry.AutoCoherence} option to 0. This will\nprevent any automatic duplicate check/replacement. If you still need to\nremove the duplicates entities, simply add @code{Coherence;} at\nstrategic locations in your @file{.geo} files (e.g. before the creation\nof curve loops, etc.)."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: a5e0ca205aefc12b65fde3ab1dcf041c40ff7814cc1c2f922e45d5ad28dd519d
#. source_hash: 273de2e1805e487e68c5358bd637c1e9b3d9592def7c2ecf0f2278a0c75bd29d
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|item|Why is my ``.geo_unrolled'' file incomplete?"
msgid "Why is my ``.geo_unrolled'' file incomplete?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 37f4f54ead387ee8138ae60471736adc39ca931cfffdd0f1ce8881e8925a9664
#. source_hash: ed1eaaf35eece0d06adebcb560d98a0fd17fe7572e65d24e0eeec7554100d167
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|paragraph|Why is my ``.geo_unrolled'' file incomplete?"
msgid "``Unrolled GEO'' files can only fully represent geometries created with\nthe built-in geometry kernel. If you want to serialize a geometry\ncreated with the OpenCASCADE geometry kernel, you should use the native\nOpenCASCADE ``.brep'' format."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: eec91709f8d5c1e43e5ef258ec310070aebccaa264872cd9422df0443a20aaeb
#. source_hash: 9ad01feac2876014f0974d32cf57454489ee6febdf78d98b960a4c0426107557
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|item|How can I display only selected parts of my model?"
msgid "How can I display only selected parts of my model?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 76b708a422a7a627fb4ade3396ba1531d9033d1c46382379a07af1a355321d81
#. source_hash: 79f2da38bab894ad01296fd9146ab450d97427b08cf46324d89847c2b7d69fc3
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|paragraph|How can I display only selected parts of my model?"
msgid "Use `Tools->Visibility'. This allows you to select elementary entities\nand physical groups, as well as mesh elements, in a variety of ways (in\na list or tree browser, by tag, interactively, or per window)."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 5fde4cd1e7d6d0ee0a6f76662279b40df1d5cbe54a6f700797f9932cb300313b
#. source_hash: da3549bf27929a969731d093216eafc54f49dd052248f2b51b278907a25a375f
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|item|Can I edit STEP/IGES/BRep models?"
msgid "Can I edit STEP/IGES/BRep models?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 71745843fd131816d71f2fe4967643697b72221c201adde9599fd0de89f7fb01
#. source_hash: 462c51f17e19586b0ceeb99098ddd13424d3b2c3501c950efcc9ff792ddb7f1b
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|paragraph|Can I edit STEP/IGES/BRep models?"
msgid "Yes: with the OpenCASCADE kernel (@code{SetFactory(\"OpenCASCADE\");}),\nload the file (@code{Merge \"file.step\";} or\n@code{ShapeFromFile(\"file.step\");}) and add the relevant scripting\ncommands after that to delete parts, create new parts or apply boolean\noperators. See\ne.g. @url{@value{GITLAB-PREFIX}/examples/boolean/import.geo,examples/boolean/import.geo}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 3d506d106c2648bb540d9b520dbc004d2c7b8b9d6c9fabf7f665ce93b9d83328
#. source_hash: 099cfe7be9f623eb87d785f1a2b714f06d08c42aa2300059d362ac2256a72eaf
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|item|Why are there surfaces missing when I export a STEP as an unrolled @file{.geo} file?"
msgid "Why are there surfaces missing when I export a STEP as an unrolled @file{.geo} file?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 66c2f92daf38bed484cb4f7dbdb6ab9814752ea161a77f668fdd4fc61da498b5
#. source_hash: c2abd5d1497ca1b7079eb6cd2cff5fff36f5c4c7ceddc2cd85cdb066faa3c2bb
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|paragraph|Why are there surfaces missing when I export a STEP as an unrolled @file{.geo} file?"
msgid "You should @emph{not} export STEP models as @file{.geo} files. By\ndesign, Gmsh never translates from one CAD format to another. The\n``unrolled GEO'' feature is there for unrolling complex GEO scripts\nwritted with the built-in geometry kernel. While it can indeed export a\nlimited subset of geometrical entities created by other CAD kernels\n(e.g. OpenCASCADE), this feature is available for debugging purposes."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: b83fd77f8eff3f009c9bc721bc871c8f6845fad24191635a2e2f85cd980f7223
#. source_hash: 51b950bfbe398b2ec8a869d3feb900a66650fda567e9b53bb9ac029d6b04b97e
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|item|How can I build modular geometries?"
msgid "How can I build modular geometries?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 35550e0ccf5df3f03d031e8e515d7f803c39687898892671102c5ebb717db27b
#. source_hash: a2220666cb092ff217bcc98356adbba58601675d3649aff57297597e9910b6fc
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|paragraph|How can I build modular geometries?"
msgid "Define common geometrical objects and options in separate files or using\n@code{Macro}, reusable in all your problem definition structures. Or use\nthe features of your language of choice and the Gmsh API."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: a22ac3e06c8ca4aee60ca9186fd3f8c60c6288ab8fd9c757af9301f7ef293de2
#. source_hash: a1b9817feb75e266f578db6d21e06145294aeb784f5d67bacab635a6100b6abc
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|item|Some files take much more time to load with Gmsh 4 compared to Gmsh 3: what's happening?"
msgid "Some files take much more time to load with Gmsh 4 compared to Gmsh 3: what's happening?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 341fc1ffb381556a688ac5b313455f9bea2164bef6216b35b82a1ad05d160c23
#. source_hash: 25a55c14277647471508deeca1ae29d34365e99ac13c8978e9973a21cb8baa05
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Geometry module questions|paragraph|Some files take much more time to load with Gmsh 4 compared to Gmsh 3: what's happening?"
msgid "In Gmsh 4, some operations (@code{Color}, @code{Show}, @code{Hide},\n@code{BoundingBox}, @code{Boundary}, @code{PointsOf}, @code{Periodic},\n@code{In} embedding constraints, ..) are now applied directly on the\ninternal Gmsh model, instead of being handled at the level of the CAD\nkernel. This implies a synchronization between the CAD kernel and the\nGmsh model. To minimize the number of synchronizations (which can become\ncostly for large models), you should always create your geometry first;\nand use these commands once the geometry has been created."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 48199d2f4c5b5c4119d34e6238b2ed3bf341f28d215b25cd16499eefce4e9459
#. source_hash: 6a48c0ab27376e250fe183e947a66aaf81b6af675051e2207224ba785526d027
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|heading|Mesh module questions"
msgid "Mesh module"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: ce8158c5c97037a151a2db1c1c6684a7c822325a5add22b45630f4e347faaabe
#. source_hash: 1c0545800c14fb68b89cbf378e088fa0eb2a1f5924c7e8ae00d4ddd4f466d178
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|What should I do when the 2D unstructured algorithm fails?"
msgid "What should I do when the 2D unstructured algorithm fails?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 078f1efdfc87d78d79b34e180b460d31ede64d89302cbc7fea3fefe1aeb23cf5
#. source_hash: a4373441da8dfe32d5e4bc4d5e3bda946f45d8b2cdcdaf73c39db0869444c3be
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|What should I do when the 2D unstructured algorithm fails?"
msgid "Verify that the curves in the model do not self-intersect. If\n`@code{Mesh.RandomFactor} * size of triangle / size of model' approaches\nmachine accuracy, increase @code{Mesh.RandomFactor}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 6e6a2098e9b4112c3f35b9adef473e46cf4920af61ec18dbb4963c55e78d0fc0
#. source_hash: 3e7ea71c82a0f2a612943a39b88ff539d4605b5ceff71dcdf8bf5c5b7cbd3864
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|What should I do when the 2D unstructured algorithm fails?#2"
msgid "If everything fails file a bug report with the version of your operating\nsystem and the full geometry."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 74056916ac1d19d7bc5a64bc4ef81f2996e6b2564fa99c8eff4d7192a701ed3a
#. source_hash: 43f15f290078f0ef0673d5d7a0de7af58b0b032d8505f6690d504f232cb29c76
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|What should I do when the 3D unstructured algorithm fails?"
msgid "What should I do when the 3D unstructured algorithm fails?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 0f5db4fd16572b7aee68abdfe864ffdbb95de774b10d020a791e7a0fe28ab398
#. source_hash: 8a05265370e630f794d31b93cedaeb14b4b835f8447e53bd969db691d9a9efd9
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|What should I do when the 3D unstructured algorithm fails?"
msgid "Verify that the surfaces in your model do not self-intersect or\npartially overlap. If they don't, try the other 3D algorithms\n(`Tool->Options->Mesh->General->3D algorithm') or try to adapt the mesh\nelement sizes in your input file so that the surface mesh better matches\nthe geometrical details of the model."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: edd5989ac0365972806c50a9394ff768849ce0d765113a669a2ce7c15fe90d72
#. source_hash: fcbc2916ed5b0b29f73df762bae1593d49b83ec17f5c0c49e6c95707b64bc0ac
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|What should I do when the 3D unstructured algorithm fails?#2"
msgid "If nothing works, file a bug report with the version of your operating\nsystem and the full geometry."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: c59f5e4df0bdf6fa97e593409224fe93832dcc95a174bd4e4a564664a9dd5c55
#. source_hash: 9901b1eb441a160be2388ce39154e12a378e247098ab07f0b09b4f08e54658f4
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|How can I only save tetrahedral elements (not triangles and lines)?"
msgid "How can I only save tetrahedral elements (not triangles and lines)?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 2f40e1ee0b9944ae4d261e946c2e38315fb0e3a607ee4de6d638f137cd1b02c8
#. source_hash: 8cdb5c48c7abb9d06f68f6fb3cd820623898fde6735aaa282bf38c9601a058eb
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|How can I only save tetrahedral elements (not triangles and lines)?"
msgid "By default, if physical groups are defined, the output mesh only\ncontains those elements that belong to physical entities. So to save\nonly 3D elements, simply define one (or more) physical volume(s) and\ndon't define any physical surfaces, physical curves or physical points."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 80676353d90e5cd30b129379c54c79de233589a747ca4e848e3abdbd33814eaf
#. source_hash: 36fa71f35d3bbc370e3d940627f923a6adefd6a197ab9fef00f8e2f48990d79f
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|How can I remove mesh nodes for geometrical construction points (centers of spheres, etc.) from output mesh file?"
msgid "How can I remove mesh nodes for geometrical construction points (centers of spheres, etc.) from output mesh file?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: a6d7cb3aed1bfe17f4df590cf030c883fc509fd1bc18a519e7d27130d6d6766c
#. source_hash: 171d0d7a570fd8296a9c79197ddbe239e938078f26ad5bb7227c3794cbe357af
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|How can I remove mesh nodes for geometrical construction points (centers of spheres, etc.) from output mesh file?"
msgid "By default Gmsh saves all the geometrical entities and their associated\nmesh. In particular, since each geometry point is meshed with a point\nelement, defined by a mesh node, the output file will contain one 0-D\nmesh element and one mesh node for each geometry point. To remove such\nelements/nodes from the mesh, simply define physical groups for the\nentities you want to save (see previous question)."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 524d9a848cee92bfcbec2bc7f1f3cccb36bcc4c314111cc38c3ec6f5f9d70ce6
#. source_hash: 131c1ae11486f1fbcea35840c9f1fbaea5072d7cc382ac59416f8a9e1e232a25
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|My 2D meshes of IGES files present gaps between surfaces"
msgid "My 2D meshes of IGES files present gaps between surfaces"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 1a5b3e36d4cadd54f425011c8454695e9624fda18fd69b3a6ca362d90769e2df
#. source_hash: 1ad43d80354b7f0a5854c2236bab68525efe87f0472d59096730535184df347c
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|My 2D meshes of IGES files present gaps between surfaces"
msgid "IGES files do not contain the topology of the model, and tolerance\nproblems can thus appear when the OpenCASCADE importer cannot identify\ntwo (close) curves as actually being identical."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 538ba90dc5fd68aa836434d1487f65f8d1c69f1a9d5d2da738aebe655c128722
#. source_hash: b749df86f0e782544a166b7d83a66052711705fdf0da5b2cb4efafe3a3d8aa9e
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|My 2D meshes of IGES files present gaps between surfaces#2"
msgid "The best solution is to @emph{not use IGES and use STEP} instead. If you\nreally have to use IGES, check that you don't have duplicate curves\n(e.g. by displaying their tags in the GUI with\n`Tools->Options->Geometry->Visibility->Curve labels'). If there are\nduplicates, try to change the geometrical tolerance and sew the faces\n(see options in `Tools->Options->Geometry->General')."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 2b65d9611df23f89c1f60fbde7ef2b771a72198cabe0f0c4657a0ee3a4a0a682
#. source_hash: be13ffd083a9d2e7d38b325d2770387e131f2dd16a02d563430ac3c9c2a92393
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|The quality of the elements generated by the 3D algorithm is very bad."
msgid "The quality of the elements generated by the 3D algorithm is very bad."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 95c6208c4cc034c741007a2100492eeb42e4e49525a8ae7cb17e682690f9b609
#. source_hash: 4a9914c8474bb3e34c61b48e1256b3a78537036f1aa5d8ff96f72f423c24efc0
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|The quality of the elements generated by the 3D algorithm is very bad."
msgid "Use `Optimize quality' in the mesh menu."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: d9fd51f684e76b31709480e6498e96bc82ed0a17c67cbbefaa412b8438915049
#. source_hash: 7904471e9dbbfb85a2f3c4b4fa5e8c7c215d8fa610cc07ac70a83bdd59e9d948
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|Non-recombined 3D extruded meshes sometimes fail."
msgid "Non-recombined 3D extruded meshes sometimes fail."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 435f31b4d6fbd1998ebb9e2560fb854a21e583b92b1c7585836dd401a627feff
#. source_hash: f1a3786ef488ef7be6dd92807241bc6a14cb28c16db2cb5be88f56d210e5bb65
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|Non-recombined 3D extruded meshes sometimes fail."
msgid "The swapping algorithm is not very clever. Try to change the surface\nmesh a bit, or recombine your mesh to generate prisms or hexahedra\ninstead of tetrahedra."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: cf9bb2330f5d8a695004709ce1109f8e674425cfceebae9546b250f5bdb6cd26
#. source_hash: 512ab5680599b3fca1beac550fdecc259bad1308cc7c41d5bd76db73c43b31d8
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|Does Gmsh automatically couple unstructured tetrahedral meshes and structured hexahedral meshed using pyramids?"
msgid "Does Gmsh automatically couple unstructured tetrahedral meshes and structured hexahedral meshed using pyramids?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: e5d63c36d6f4bf9da377b2f92f042b64b88b64a18cc604cce67142182007cdd0
#. source_hash: 44720cf9dfde8b35de0badbc609985c19b0f834011efb2f2d4ab95f5581dc4ff
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|Does Gmsh automatically couple unstructured tetrahedral meshes and structured hexahedral meshed using pyramids?"
msgid "Yes, but only if pyramids need to be created on a single side of the\nquadrangular surface mesh."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: abd84c03dfb11e0c44e9e85913f4b8646908dfbab495678a79f8a0ffaa29455f
#. source_hash: efb5d462e9a47505f3a4936f29c5b1f7e8ab34cfd1579c67a0dac0fa7178f308
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|Can I explicitly assign region tags to extruded layers?"
msgid "Can I explicitly assign region tags to extruded layers?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: e96b67ac9a82e73b233de7d0110dfeeda74fd3b26d7d3b220435edb3380fbfcf
#. source_hash: 11f069f242ef92eeeac4df18aaf2ef99f65dcefafc3581a782046e4c2d1f2836
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|Can I explicitly assign region tags to extruded layers?"
msgid "No, this feature has been removed in Gmsh 2.0. You must use the\nstandard entity tag instead."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 69b808c37431b6a91e7f2d8871d4a12dc15456d01bba6ee9c3a54b3edcbd29e3
#. source_hash: 12abb7943a7a07f564a00e90f33a7cbf7f118bfa95d52912c1eb47e1a01b454c
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|Did you remove the elliptic mesh generator in Gmsh 2.0?"
msgid "Did you remove the elliptic mesh generator in Gmsh 2.0?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 39c4c645dc2128419bfd37a63ef28fabe0339739875975b2f3bf40c218510a2e
#. source_hash: 0803d0e17c76fb4fbda8c0c3ae4b5810e92b4cd91aabada3c51627d0c6923fab
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|Did you remove the elliptic mesh generator in Gmsh 2.0?"
msgid "Yes. You can achieve the same result by using the transfinite\nalgorithm with smoothing (e.g., with @code{Mesh.Smoothing = 10})."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 7ee34f7e9aa26c9c298593c7f51bcb38fb1bb0f33086f45a7a6ffcf0d057823f
#. source_hash: 99e1be34a64b443c0477b4bb8e277446dadd5c23a20787b590a0813169dcd4a2
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|Does Gmsh support curved elements?"
msgid "Does Gmsh support curved elements?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: f61964149cf6bf22261731e7460bec2dff14c8bb42fd73406fe71b04a84737df
#. source_hash: 891b48142335d74e1afa26cbfaa9374c1591c374228ea05abbc3ae0677338d92
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|Does Gmsh support curved elements?"
msgid "Yes, just choose the appropriate order in the mesh menu after the mesh\nis completed. High-order optimization tools are also available in the\nmesh menu. You can select the order on the command line with e.g.\n@code{-order 2}, and activcate high-order optimization with\n@code{-optimize_ho}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 1b0e1bb29e99a45659770552333d5a8dd6885c518a88e57b281f957c7c66c22a
#. source_hash: cf6446ba226faed52a1016dc34fd01e4314ad56502028971d271c7f16771edca
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|Can I import an existing surface mesh in Gmsh and use it to build a 3D mesh?"
msgid "Can I import an existing surface mesh in Gmsh and use it to build a 3D mesh?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 40df85153280de8eaa5450fadb0ca2e0b8530064d2ff84d322b0184ad50e072b
#. source_hash: 4e3e82464f2fc81aadec5627373a93fd2e8ea7357a30c78e2f1b76b5eff32455
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|Can I import an existing surface mesh in Gmsh and use it to build a 3D mesh?"
msgid "Yes, you can import a surface mesh in any one of the supported mesh file\nformats, define a volume, and mesh it. For an example see\n@url{@value{GITLAB-PREFIX}/examples/simple_geo/sphere-discrete.geo,examples/simple_geo/sphere-discrete.geo}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: dfafec6e3bd0fcbbd01f2e83fba28cb70bdbd2e207ca41313a360aceff1e0c7f
#. source_hash: a3dee2255f747779b48285840db344b7b24a68fd7d501f616b4b51149b62ae5d
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|How do I define boundary conditions or material properties in Gmsh?"
msgid "How do I define boundary conditions or material properties in Gmsh?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: b1bc284eb4f533d71e52d290a4e245f4cbc7a7839fe82280cb164d972908171b
#. source_hash: c2c08c01c0efb1625040a187fb5f62cdc40e127f55c666fe4a849ff3bd0439fc
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|How do I define boundary conditions or material properties in Gmsh?"
msgid "By design, Gmsh does not try to incorporate every possible definition of\nboundary conditions or material properties---this is a job best left to\nthe solver. Instead, Gmsh provides a simple mechanism to tag groups of\nelements, and it is up to the solver to interpret these tags as boundary\nconditions, materials, etc. Associating tags with elements in Gmsh is\ndone by defining physical groups (Physical Points, Physical Curves,\nPhysical Surfaces and Physical Volumes). See the reference manual as\nwell as the tutorials (in particular @ref{t1}) for a detailed\ndescription and some examples."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 7dc848396e84f2b813de5e09e7881ad6dfe7e1d4cefbea829cafd486e0e7e95d
#. source_hash: 7a55c07c3e3f199a5c90b65f385406fb1f954b80400f93855760db43bfad5ad0
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|How do I define boundary conditions or material properties in Gmsh?#2"
msgid "The Gmsh API can be used to build sophisticated interactive workflows\nwhere the definition of boundary conditions and material properties can\nbe fully tailored to your preferred solver. For an example see\n@url{@value{GITLAB-PREFIX}/examples/api/prepro.py,examples/api/prepro.py}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 6715033f1659050765a18ec118165cfa2968587b69aaa67615dc9667d65d541a
#. source_hash: b4a9f7c45c218e178dace6a61444e0cfe78306083947f98cd3dbba59e4fd7c43
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|How can I display only the mesh associated with selected geometrical entities?"
msgid "How can I display only the mesh associated with selected geometrical entities?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 1856b60c2f5b73dfb837e8ca870bd80a3ea757539626ca3fd0c43861e7a77161
#. source_hash: e0d9299cf451638858eddb226395ee8348628665f9c52d3063c6a29eb617a597
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|How can I display only the mesh associated with selected geometrical entities?"
msgid "See ``How can I display only selected parts of my model?''."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: ff8a2ba66f8647f0ce9a00a48fed9d80226a7bf2acda4fd2b11bf51a33109ac0
#. source_hash: d3386a27be2e5c8de3ba788122bb4ad64ec6ea97f61d345829676be4a0e6b72c
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|How can I ``explore'' a mesh (for example, to see inside a complex structure)?"
msgid "How can I ``explore'' a mesh (for example, to see inside a complex structure)?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: f3c691c7c42e1188817eb77b37d45e5654549ba69b326bee2e07ca81bc135884
#. source_hash: 00bb6cb3ef4a55a5a9aa7e7d1419811b68252a83ebd8432ef853d44b9dfeaa51
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|How can I ``explore'' a mesh (for example, to see inside a complex structure)?"
msgid "You can use `Tools->Clipping' to clip the region of interest. You can\ndefine up to 6 clipping planes in Gmsh (i.e., enough to define a\n``cube'' inside your model) and each plane can clip either the geometry,\nthe mesh, the post-processing views, or any combination of the above.\nThe clipping planes are defined using the four coefficients A,B,C,D of\nthe equation A*x+B*y+C*y+D=0, which can be adjusted interactively by\ndragging the mouse in the input fields."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 87a565461037d3e45fc70b43f05cbcca5442d8d42ea981bfafd70f02616f77e3
#. source_hash: 3f3b310d40de3b2fb51e1423bed5b29220dd17e0eefafeccb0fe565008ae03db
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|What is the signification of SICN, Gamma and SIGE in Tools->Statistics?"
msgid "What is the signification of SICN, Gamma and SIGE in Tools->Statistics?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: d944c5b6dcc23f04a8ed251b49e63d81195a779236406b23a51fa842d2fd0ad9
#. source_hash: 951967d3312fecdcf59891b42cd4957d9f541ef0fbee6d4f3947b0db529a119b
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|What is the signification of SICN, Gamma and SIGE in Tools->Statistics?"
msgid "They measure the quality of the elements' shape in a mesh. They are defined for\nall 2D and 3D element types, except Gamma which is not defined for hexes and pyramids.\nThe `S-' of SICN and SIGE stands for `Signed'. Those two measures actually give two information\non an element: Their absolute value gives the element's shape quality while their sign\ngives the element validity. In other words, negative values of SICN and SIGE\nindicate tangled elements. The pure quality part of the measures take values\nbetween 0 and 1, with 1 corresponding to the best shape."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 4e1a5a78e3c7dde11cf36f3bcf9e81a28d8574a9d1061ff1bd290312925a45f4
#. source_hash: e3b924a11d9d585535e75518b15a63a5ed633176d3c2a56c4517a4aec0781bdf
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|What is the signification of SICN, Gamma and SIGE in Tools->Statistics?#2"
msgid "The measures are computed for 3D elements if any, otherwise for 2D elements. Given values\nare the average, the minimum and the maximum among elements.\nGraphs plot the number of elements vs. the quality measure."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 960e0989bb82f8384aeea18e96c57a3360e8657a22bbb03519c184f73d97165a
#. source_hash: fa994251eeeecbd801c720224a930dbaca18ecddd248c049dece36a1967f6294
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|What is the signification of SICN, Gamma and SIGE in Tools->Statistics?#3"
msgid "Meaning of the measures:"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: adaa7c2544cf580200ae80a6bd4dc5ca8957ceb8a23f04118d4762a7b413000a
#. source_hash: c46efb82a46f1655bd9e5c9bcb1b6b1b6616d0f9f04a530c29ca981a6ef7cc51
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|9923414888be12b7b4f25ca8c3c4a83ff02e811b5ec2658054639b7a9c0b483a"
msgid "`(S-)ICN' stands  for (Signed-) Inverse Condition Number. Low-ICN elements influence\nnegatively the condition number of the stiffness matrix which, as a result, can\nintroduce roundoff errors, or significantly reduce the convergence speed of iterative methods."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: b5ab6c72d172d578b114c5c7a42c973d1878d5394eba7917de863b96f062b05c
#. source_hash: 1d1a2be57f4b8b690720c0c8d6722949383e0ee353d56f1e830f01b8202a984c
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|a50dacf2bcff1451bb40b59a3342a33f8229f5588f431e74a8d041edfced9939"
msgid "`(S-)IGE' stands  for (Signed-) Inverse Gradient Error. Low-IGE elements influence\nnegatively the error on the gradient of the FE solution. For hexahedra, the measure\nis the well-known \"scaled Jacobian\" as defined in the hexahedral community."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: ef8f5983ebabbb07bbfc8218d8b9365e83295d2f9e12f34cdd4e7d77861f76f3
#. source_hash: 5dcce7a11980945e43bb18e2eb67e41c03469dbd02325bed7184e27747ec2789
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|6b69bbad4d1de416baa6402b4c63995bc0232617b4e3b4f91e71df0d28a90002"
msgid "For triangles and tetrahedra, `Gamma' is equal to the ratio\n\"inscribed radius / circumscribed radius\", which is a measure that is largely used\nin the community of simplicial meshes. This form is closely related to the ICN measure."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: b6cd356fb4193d5cd5d1d3bee61bca6ed49fd1ece5870304c88a5aa531e45ee1
#. source_hash: 2f5d631e45f0b87c978b4deb011f9c5711158721bf82e0cbc9b47144da282938
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|0c1e875acfd91d81fae71af84cdaae533d7cffb1c043fa7c61e4c38aa9c83f06"
msgid "For quadrangles and prisms, `Gamma' is computed on the worst corner using the\nformula [1 - abs(r)] where r is proportional to the difference between the corner angle\nand the perfect (solid) angle, that is pi/2 for quads and pi/3 for prisms. This form is closely\nrelated to the IGE measure."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: d27989c54863a111fc18411d9934d076955e6f0616dc50426106a650a3c36112
#. source_hash: fcb3ce0a40d621226c5db60dceb57991d7ea709c2e812df1c15a732b267a3347
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|1146d3e3eb273491c980c14bbc2d8ba99162448fb34e94ea2e53cfb6434a7115"
msgid "For the exact definitions, see\n@url{@value{GITLAB-PREFIX}/src/geo/MElement.cpp,src/geo/MElement.cpp}, and, for\nthe ICN and IGE measures, see the paper\nA. Johnen, C. Geuzaine, T. Toulorge and J.-F. Remacle,\n@url{https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0010448518301015,\n\"Efficient computation of the minimum of shape quality measures on curvilinear finite elements\"},\nComputer-Aided Design 103, pp. 24-33, 2018."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 7780f8fa72477875a9c8d0cab13843d8f2356574b592a4fe39b121879e4b1d81
#. source_hash: c22e5ec0b5425affb4f0eaf00802ecaa2cb3218b7880772580d1c97510c0413d
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|How can I save a mesh file with a given (e.g. older) MSH file format version?"
msgid "How can I save a mesh file with a given (e.g. older) MSH file format version?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 17dc0bfaf23bef327cbe9083ed3db4cb70ae954b10964e69b6cf1baaaf2196aa
#. source_hash: fca99a3a740392f8c5af687889595ecfdfff3ff00afe5330377caa74773279e4
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|e9908b9860ec3a3af9d5f8d1e1cb40175bcaee739b0cd2d83f73370068a0920c"
msgid "In the GUI: open `File->Export', enter your @file{filename.msh} and then\npick the version in the dropdown menu."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: a8998349664ddd5057e3a9f1167156925d916206f114e15d2635b371ee01ee25
#. source_hash: 9c0a0681b0070db6bc74fddac736a76ae2be629c18740ea2cec50d95816f9f79
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|5c53c2f3271bedd5b76d3d9645a6943e7ce683b79c3aa8642ed90333141233ca"
msgid "On the command line: use the @code{-format} option (e.g. @code{gmsh\nfile.geo -format msh2 -2})."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: f7233a4f640a4e401e731b10fbb9a71b03a49121b590d22c331be969020cfbf0
#. source_hash: c026970e8d888a2c6290f2490a9b297e3694500b2c1d63b3703acda8b59de78e
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|d5030d8b4002b8cb6168719c53e225e459e41ae7267604e8f5a2317eaa1c7d8e"
msgid "In a @file{.geo} script: add the line @code{Mesh.MshFileVersion = x.y;}\nfor any version number @code{x.y}. You can also save this in your\ndefault options."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 4e7b85ed434e61bc2ad12c5087efb193805501d0e921bf1bd0f08a162c2fdf93
#. source_hash: 893b1f28a6ca24ebbcfabfa487576be4c442e42296d5c598006b4c9a6c4a3b6f
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|99272de268496532a8d5145f3de88a164361416b7afb45b2b67973cec3c60833"
msgid "In the API: @code{gmsh::option::setNumber(\"Mesh.MshFileVersion\", x.y)}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: f2efbe37ce79d8889a517f0a08ff4687bc4ebd2b3a58e5374fdfe518229ed0a2
#. source_hash: 8419894ffba32e1b9e1842d851522f124e5b87cbccf739059c52a99715674311
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|17533500dcad36d5c018e07e475cc273cd614211ec5f203bcd4d5daf6dd7994d"
msgid "As an alternative method, you can also not specify the format\nexplicitly, and just choose a filename with the @code{.msh2} or\n@code{.msh4} extension."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 8dbcf3807078ce4909b20476dfeffe772bad5db166ebf62a0053b6d2becc834a
#. source_hash: ee570b3c0eac2fa150bfdfdbd0eb08b87a1c27024b97c223a52b5e3ff1b4406f
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|Why isn't neighboring element information stored in the MSH file?"
msgid "Why isn't neighboring element information stored in the MSH file?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: eb3ac76f2f84cf7d7b7f2fc47fc0fb570a141434829b7dca26f3ae1a43a41994
#. source_hash: 9b99077770e7f3130efe18532328946288f16388ba8803d33bac8d7d22424f88
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|Why isn't neighboring element information stored in the MSH file?"
msgid "Each numerical method has its own requirements: it might need\nneighboring elements connected by a node, an edge or a face; it might\nrequire a single layer or multiple layers; it should include elements of\nlower dimension (boundaries) or not, go across geometrical entities or\nmesh partitions or not, etc. Given the number of possibilities,\ngenerating the appropriate information is thus best performed in the\nnumerical solver itself. The Gmsh API makes these computations easy: see\ntutorial @ref{x7} and\n@url{@value{GITLAB-PREFIX}/examples/api/neighbors.py,examples/api/neighbors.py}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 305633a560a139654b9eef8105bd9c44b64d41212eb82018eaa3085074c28ecf
#. source_hash: a2323b911132789c65f7287860adfef12ec331c6add7007a829eb81cdbfcedd7
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|item|Could mesh edges/faces be stored in the MSH file?"
msgid "Could mesh edges/faces be stored in the MSH file?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 8308c4dec946e66bc3665c4d0ca7c3317fd739dfa0760a1f0ab7a931dc697b5b
#. source_hash: 8eb140e6866e2e89c022f3cdc94e6db3935bb7cd38c5c3be384aeb35a7bc97e8
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Mesh module questions|paragraph|Could mesh edges/faces be stored in the MSH file?"
msgid "Edge/faces can be easily generated from the information already\navailable in the file (i.e. nodes and elements), or through the Gmsh\nAPI: see tutorial @ref{x7},\n@url{@value{GITLAB-PREFIX}/examples/api/edges.cpp,examples/api/edges.cpp} and\n@url{@value{GITLAB-PREFIX}/examples/api/faces.cpp,examples/api/faces.cpp}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 35f072a47d67fb6c0631932d8f7bce1d4694d6cd10bfbeb69e0aa6dce7f8b88c
#. source_hash: 7ec3268882a65a02f8e16a7c14265faff5d4f0c2aed1c51531cfcd612c215181
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Solver module questions|heading|Solver module questions"
msgid "Solver module"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 5a10e34cb0c982e85b67637ec46cdb71ec47ba59c3447d9e4fa1d240ee93b04e
#. source_hash: 614c5cfc4a86e035fa31ea672e9dc91370fd5e54c5c88ff7c6211143aef413fc
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Solver module questions|item|How do I integrate my own solver with Gmsh?"
msgid "How do I integrate my own solver with Gmsh?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 2beb5229062ed884dd1910db6a784296bda9d2f0b9a7847fd9522fc25b142590
#. source_hash: c78062ff70cd6d1b8e6305d904694fdfddcf160db65a75c9cbf31358f34c6c6e
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Solver module questions|paragraph|How do I integrate my own solver with Gmsh?"
msgid "Gmsh uses the ONELAB interface (@url{http://www.onelab.info}) to\ninteract with external solvers. See @ref{Solver module}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 00719beb6ae1b0dba1082af4cb08efc528414f27626c34ab6c3c7ec592e713fa
#. source_hash: c46b6c7f89223d7d069b6b697a58f449b1f32410a04fa1eef4fdc08c62bffffb
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Solver module questions|item|Can I launch Gmsh from my solver (instead of launching my solver from Gmsh) in order to monitor a solution?"
msgid "Can I launch Gmsh from my solver (instead of launching my solver from Gmsh) in order to monitor a solution?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: f733c06e77cf6fa3148f0412234de27edc85d061a9bd2cce9ef58e0c3cd06d62
#. source_hash: b9977b440821132a1659fea61dcf362509a74c32bb1e9b1d0dd780cfe52f695a
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Solver module questions|paragraph|Can I launch Gmsh from my solver (instead of launching my solver from Gmsh) in order to monitor a solution?"
msgid "Using the Gmsh API, you can directly embed Gmsh in your own solver, use\nONELAB for interactive parameter definition and modification, and create\nvisualization data on the fly. See e.g.\n@url{@value{GITLAB-PREFIX}/examples/api/prepro.py,prepro.py},\n@url{@value{GITLAB-PREFIX}/examples/api/custom_gui.py,custom_gui.py},\n@url{@value{GITLAB-PREFIX}/examples/api/custom_gui.cpp,custom_gui.cpp}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: e86d986911b53307bc4242003d36946a2cf5e270b9656ac3096151151548a810
#. source_hash: bc22d24aaba5d5ef4cbac548dc41b52972139bf660e426bcfa7d1d8215e46b36
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Solver module questions|paragraph|Can I launch Gmsh from my solver (instead of launching my solver from Gmsh) in order to monitor a solution?#2"
msgid "Another (rather crude) approach if to launch the Gmsh app everytime you\nwant to visualize something (a simple C program showing how to do this\nis given in\n@url{@value{GITLAB-PREFIX}/utils/misc/callgmsh.c,utils/misc/callgmsh.c})."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 23096b8c3d13cce7df1be8ec9484000a099a07e800b8b5445faa7ed278b930da
#. source_hash: 0e94d375c9ed6d461f935b635bbe2591a5a048be80d5827f15e63aabb72f6984
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Solver module questions|paragraph|Can I launch Gmsh from my solver (instead of launching my solver from Gmsh) in order to monitor a solution?#3"
msgid "Yet another approach is to modify your program so that it can\ncommunicate with Gmsh through ONELAB over a socket. Select `Always\nlisten to incoming connection requests' in the Gmsh solver option panel\n(or run gmsh with the @code{-listen} command line switch), and Gmsh will\nalways listen for your program on the given socket (or on the\n@code{Solver.SocketName} if no socket is specified)."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: c10d903258bce3775f6e5f2fc4d3e519981bcd0aa20d8762756a0e40aecf983f
#. source_hash: 47d9e314e6d275d545956a846b3d276625e849c4356498a6f00771da242489c6
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|heading|Post-processing module questions"
msgid "Post-processing module"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 59a1799fcf6d1fd56574006983cb4d104689458ff65862cf9f6d1ff316a82925
#. source_hash: ed54dbb175ea60cb3722b0ff5343e052e448a95aa2faa32bebb1c5c2de2e7412
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|item|How do I compute a section of a plot?"
msgid "How do I compute a section of a plot?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: a578a8464b3e51a66a6b13860c74c1c2bbc9cb267a4daec668ccf5bd9f55a230
#. source_hash: 8d8bf7cd81e4630355bbdd7042be71e6bdff3b55c1316ef4d1f06b3e0ccff684
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|How do I compute a section of a plot?"
msgid "Use `Tools->Plugins->Cut Plane'."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: b8a78ecfc4eb7de116289ef305786bb2a0e1be97d0c0ba30bffb07d01f007a07
#. source_hash: 90281f22f1ee2b13845cc247e00723683478fb7a8089a237864b9609ec4d00ae
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|item|Can I save an isosurface to a file?"
msgid "Can I save an isosurface to a file?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 729e3fd976eef8cf4c98571ee03048f3ae6246d688ede10174fccfbb86df06a8
#. source_hash: 313db389775cc0c8b516917ddd158ae4c102bc5abbd240ff895e0d0d7e1c92f8
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|Can I save an isosurface to a file?"
msgid "Yes: first run `Tools->Plugins->Isosurface' to extract the isosurface,\nthen use `View->Export' to save the new view."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 54fef180ac42b9420ea297ae39617963b6d14a9cb9889ff7dffa42db6af54096
#. source_hash: 3e471ff1c6a7210ac22021dc64cadcf6d4cf35df37af4e6b218785bfd648d842
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|item|Can Gmsh generate isovolumes?"
msgid "Can Gmsh generate isovolumes?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: a105c83c5cedcbb27c96b59783cea063cbbe82fc47d80bf713975b191d3d581e
#. source_hash: 151e247833de7f350ebaba4290f38631e435d7d9199638c810e31cfccba4416e
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|Can Gmsh generate isovolumes?"
msgid "Yes, with the CutMap plugin (set the ExtractVolume option to -1 or 1\nto extract the negative or positive levelset)."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: b9dd1e37e5f0174bb1a38b2148f45d90a1e8c5eb55caf1e762f3753fc87fece8
#. source_hash: a8af5e78120f5aff1ba7596a7afed10f7f1840dd57689859e556fbb822a21924
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|item|How do I animate my plots?"
msgid "How do I animate my plots?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 0cebbbdc4c7e0b4af04870ec41090ef6338385f7223f9b54da817918fb8be8dd
#. source_hash: b5b445eb5f2a56b873211e574c4117fd38a2e762c1612c1a13816c64d87792d5
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|How do I animate my plots?"
msgid "If the views contain multiple time steps, you can press the `play'\nbutton at the bottom of the graphic window, or change the time step by\nhand in the view option panel. You can also use the left and right\narrow keys on your keyboard to change the time step in all visible\nviews in real time."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 38804b271f83e5dcc54769fd63deeb803d0d3bf9def9266b51f7a770921e3d34
#. source_hash: c8192dab8fd6eb8b5939529f6aa36908cba30511a611bafa6209489e5dd2cc18
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|How do I animate my plots?#2"
msgid "If you want to loop through different views instead of time steps, you\ncan use the `Loop through views instead of time steps' option in the\nview option panel, or use the up and down arrow keys on your keyboard."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: fd668fc2f3889b20cbe45c350dc74bdafac9d7600a15b93d7711d8ad6891619a
#. source_hash: 29c837800ff2c0f061670746b044c7f73ce763eb8d4daa2a075b3363ca7e9c74
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|item|How do I visualize a deformed mesh?"
msgid "How do I visualize a deformed mesh?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 65bdfff203283372029a50c08bb1cd74025b735707b309db1c74fdb1fb3ad0b4
#. source_hash: 957391ffb45b5795f509ad46f37ab1306f9db9e8c53cafd11f1be8a247e20c5a
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|How do I visualize a deformed mesh?"
msgid "Load a vector view containing the displacement field, and set `Vector\ndisplay' to `Displacement' in `View->Options->Aspect'. If the\ndisplacement is too small (or too large), you can scale it with the\n`Displacement factor' option. (Remember that you can drag the mouse in\nall numeric input fields to slide the value!)"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: c1281a5ce4287bf27650ad69ae4bf67e7b00188277a95783d4ae2278eda19114
#. source_hash: 7e819181cda0ffe8a91750488807fb62b8568a27ef9bc55be1a7a51053d62ad6
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|How do I visualize a deformed mesh?#2"
msgid "Another option is to use the `General transformation expressions' (in\nView->Options->Offset) on a scalar view, with the displacement map\nselected as the data source."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 6db7c14eedd879e1c816fb2fa50cabbce03a2a77ba41e454ddc2d503865e04cb
#. source_hash: 5333834553e6cdd71b4ba8d3e7303fc95ed537bd59efed7a79497b4aef817208
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|item|Can I visualize a field on a deformed mesh?"
msgid "Can I visualize a field on a deformed mesh?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 1f3671355f0dc998aa28bd1869815f6c8bf3f5a3e1b852708c0189df058a0b35
#. source_hash: 01658869d967b1ee9f951d859b9372983a5a2d8ddecdc36b08eb21736f6aca06
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|Can I visualize a field on a deformed mesh?"
msgid "Yes, there are several ways to do that."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: d6c0628d72309ab18ed54226fec8edbcc5f860a413877a4fc55afa4a3b1b338b
#. source_hash: c2e604ed4579617a0afb6ed32346a021c4ed7b56171c61aa01418080c0ea3629
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|Can I visualize a field on a deformed mesh?#2"
msgid "The easiest is to load two views: the first one containing a\ndisplacement field (a vector view that will be used to deform the\nmesh), and the second one containing the field you want to display\n(this view has to contain the same number of elements as the\ndisplacement view). You should then set `Vector display' to\n`Displacement' in the first view, as well as set `Data source' to\npoint to the second view. (You might want to make the second view\ninvisible, too. If you want to amplify or decrease the amount of\ndeformation, just modify the `Displacement factor' option.)"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 775b2b41449bb07b99737e2746302695413a3695f3caaa91f63f2c39525426fc
#. source_hash: 95345a5e487821ee86134608cedea9ac696ed65c9a8f14f9addf9b6fb363651c
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|Can I visualize a field on a deformed mesh?#3"
msgid "Another solution is to use the `General transformation expressions'\n(in `View->Options->Offset') on the field you want to display, with\nthe displacement map selected as the data source."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: dc5323290f7770531a1e0aa5ddd06ff255fa138a073546d1499f307e8c3fc9b7
#. source_hash: b4d97b9e4819d7eee83ba61ec1d799c2b1874a5d4b81e2485d6e80e228d7578b
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|Can I visualize a field on a deformed mesh?#4"
msgid "And yet another solution is to use the Warp plugin."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: f19b3d9bf84d9fc832e1e24e1c810d0d8d68f39712de2bfc778766182a909244
#. source_hash: ef7854182791681551f50030fe0baaa52f4703419973e3293f1a6b14ec139d01
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|item|Can I color the arrows representing a vector field with data from a scalar field?"
msgid "Can I color the arrows representing a vector field with data from a scalar field?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: ab596d2ccac4cbc38c73551db1a60596af6ec07aa3c11f520989995df914ad0d
#. source_hash: 98027875d833c23f2f214edda2c868f48585bc7df53f3dc767a50f137f36d1c9
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|Can I color the arrows representing a vector field with data from a scalar field?"
msgid "Yes: load both the vector and the scalar fields (the two views must\nhave the same number of elements) and, in the vector field options,\nselect the scalar view in `Data source'."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 0bab1053f5548e15dbb839db83cef3f2e879930455932802720e730bce83db34
#. source_hash: c9cd11bc9e2c3435d08810b3ccb587b4c9d2312c218a82616e9fc653f950d45a
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|item|Can I color isovalue surfaces with data from another scalar view?"
msgid "Can I color isovalue surfaces with data from another scalar view?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 98b32b9b040a21f0dd0669b979ca10771c11404253d6b7c6b9616d56e9e43f8c
#. source_hash: 39fe1f0344cce78d1aa6ea38df1a4c24d328ecc626d1f3a77e816b8901e94481
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|Can I color isovalue surfaces with data from another scalar view?"
msgid "Yes, using either the CutMap plugin (with the `dView' option) or the\nEvaluate plugin."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 12c33cea3a8dcc5267765461e12192d5b703269e0befe1d2755fc867a65abe78
#. source_hash: aa1acff40419363f47cddf3cf14e6a436bf4fde50d657f8917f13c5113e54ec8
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|item|Is there a way to save animations?"
msgid "Is there a way to save animations?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: e0f286f810d28e092ec980e8529d3c123f6e3ae7f7c7e98db842e4e7df0706c2
#. source_hash: c7e5b0ab1ccc7793a67a545f8c623a2d3680cdf6f1cde857a1eb17578cb21e3d
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|Is there a way to save animations?"
msgid "You can save simple MPEG animations directly from the `File->Export'\nmenu. For other formats you should write a script. Have a look at\n@ref{t8} or\n@url{@value{GITLAB-PREFIX}/examples/post_processing/anim.script,examples/post_processing/anim.script}\nfor some examples."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: f5d6adef90cb8bc7b22e4ac597d74bcfe73195e6fe33c1b679e73ef3e7cb965b
#. source_hash: 61663f2273d3b78c8a0508e53550c245a837445ba0e775fa7d46092a20d04fbe
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|item|Is there a way to visualize only certain components of vector/tensor fields?"
msgid "Is there a way to visualize only certain components of vector/tensor fields?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 0f62ce76ca8a25cf848ad91bd5d311cb85c876d7ddcd143c764b1c5a02f957df
#. source_hash: b51f6338fd204520c4628aa93fde03031bd447d8f4de488d3b622b21199ff833
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|Is there a way to visualize only certain components of vector/tensor fields?"
msgid "Yes, by using either the ``Force field'' options in\n`Tools->Options->View->Visibility', or by using\n`Tools->Plugins->MathEval'."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: ca856fef551e2901152fbf3ed883ed284770607f6290a635b606075f67166ea4
#. source_hash: 3c2b1891729ec9a2ef650c78cf6d869bd63809d88619a26290235949645093f7
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|item|Can I do arithmetic operations on a view? Can I perform operations involving different views?"
msgid "Can I do arithmetic operations on a view? Can I perform operations involving different views?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: cf83ab2075f2cd0940783a94f883526597e9925b4a9c2608d30b314bda1f3bdd
#. source_hash: c291973352b9832b538b103c2246ca0eef47f4b879c93298f96e5b51b80feb8e
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|Can I do arithmetic operations on a view? Can I perform operations involving different views?"
msgid "Yes, with the Evaluate plugin."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: e4215697ddacacffc7a12ee4447290d12b1b52657268c1f407d08a328ba54cd0
#. source_hash: 99db1812f5be32398f3eb46950bc83c60d05976f6c0a040a743e5c3833a87756
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|item|Some plugins seem to create empty views. What's wrong?"
msgid "Some plugins seem to create empty views. What's wrong?"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: f9b55096789e3e88d6e5e643f667af0cead13b4cc7899b7f920644f9a65f939b
#. source_hash: e45361d90a26c66fa07fb9940554724405f85d7c9dc379645910d69bc0db6f7d
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|Some plugins seem to create empty views. What's wrong?"
msgid "There can be several reasons:"
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 521bd1fb1be884b2a9ebb4ba7e47fcff253c0c89598640ae2ecd379d8282f352
#. source_hash: 424d2f60325d9672d891d13ba8ecc649eafab13958ab792889bce2e65849ebc9
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|10a5a03a45dbcf7ced7ee8e560445aca9c6ac0c36a85f5359ba213cd7f7c36d7"
msgid "the plugin might be written for specific element types only (for\nexample, only for scalar triangles or tetrahedra). In that case, you\nshould transform your view before running the plugin (you can use\n@code{Plugin(DecomposeinSimplex)} to transform all quads, hexas, prisms\nand pyramids into triangles and tetrahedra)."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: ceb83d3761ec3531f1cb46b87f9d70a3c6a354e41c073f4639b5fa2d038f9de2
#. source_hash: 9cec07be8566ec373056b135e84f5f245be0ae61ef47a960b209fa1b22aa04c8
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|bd1475eb06504147bb7b02ee7cc2d8928e632fe3f70b6545d7c8cc5cb51ffb92"
msgid "the plugin might expect a mesh while all you provide is a point\ncloud. In 2D, you can use @code{Plugin(Triangulate)} to transform a\npoint cloud into a triangulated surface. In 3D you can use\n@code{Plugin(Tetrahedralize)}."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: b2a0f652490124716cdb275a97d30c32ba4af7b7ad173c3414def3be1a95c5cc
#. source_hash: ac980fcd4c988b73dca2150990e06cb0085f20a97839516236bc1b64d09b96f8
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|34a4920f94c87e979c1485e484fb3c96f389c632984837436c663b823574c4da"
msgid "the input parameters are out of range."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: dd49f693e1235095e8f917210966f968e6f31591fb5b3baa1ab9e5a5daaa3d9c
#. source_hash: 90641440a5b773c7eeecf5b11be06c4a7ad2f705196f2a677fb7f6aac080f183
#: doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi
msgctxt "doc/texinfo/gmsh.texi|Post-processing module questions|paragraph|ff95f986550fdbb2b80e000a4f92fbcb6a91e193e33e42eef02e1286e401127f"
msgid "In any case, you can automatically remove all empty views with\n`View->Remove->Empty Views' in the GUI, or with @code{Delete Empty\nViews;} in a script."
msgstr ""

#. unit_id: 719be075454dda82cdef37dd9f2786a02681732a8782aa8cfdd199ecf9f7d42c
